OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026", "title": "Topological triviality of mu-constant families of surface singularities", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/main.pdf", "family": "064", "family_title": "Topological triviality of <i>μ</i>-constant surface singularities", "abstract_html": "We prove that every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in ℂ<sup>3</sup> with constant Milnor number is topologically right-trivial. This gives a positive answer to the surface case of the <i>μ</i>-constant problem. The trivialization fixes the parameter and the origin section, and preserves the defining functions.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking the disk and the germ representatives, there is a homeomorphism germ \\( \\Phi(x,t)=(\\phi_t(x),t) \\) such that \\( \\phi_t(0)=0,\\qquad \\phi_0=\\operatorname{id}, \\qquad f_t(\\phi_t(x))=f_0(x). \\) Both \\(\\Phi\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\subset\\mathbb C\\) be a disk centered at \\(0\\), and let\n  \\[\n  F:(\\mathbb C^3\\times\\Delta,\\{0\\}\\times\\Delta)\\longrightarrow(\\mathbb C,0)\n  \\]\n  be holomorphic.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(f_t(x)=F(x,t)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(t\\in\\Delta\\), assume that \\(f_t(0)=0\\), that \\(f_t\\) has an isolated critical point at \\(0\\), and that\n  \\[\n  \\mu(f_t,0)=\\mu(f_0,0),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mu(f_t,0)=\n  \\dim_{\\mathbb C}\n  \\frac{\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb C^3,0}}\n       {(\\partial f_t/\\partial x_1,\\partial f_t/\\partial x_2,\\partial f_t/\\partial x_3)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking the disk and the germ representatives, there is a homeomorphism germ\n\\[\n\\Phi(x,t)=(\\phi_t(x),t)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\phi_t(0)=0,\\qquad \\phi_0=\\operatorname{id},\n\\qquad f_t(\\phi_t(x))=f_0(x).\n\\]\nBoth \\(\\Phi\\) and \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\) are jointly continuous on neighborhoods of the origin section over the original disk.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the proof labeled Section \\(10.1\\) in the paper.</p><p>If the central multiplicity is two, or the central surface \\(X_0=\\{f_0=0\\}\\) is log canonical, Proposition \\(10\\) proves the theorem.</p><p>Otherwise the central surface is non-log-canonical. Proposition \\(7\\) proves constancy of its logarithmic invariant. Proposition \\(8\\) supplies, after a finite disk base change, a smooth simultaneous resolution with relative normal-crossing exceptional divisor.</p><p>The fixed-ball analytic and transversality hypotheses follow from Proposition \\(1\\). Proposition \\(9\\) constructs the jointly continuous ambient right-trivialization after the base change and removes the finite disk cover.</p><p>The resulting maps preserve the parameter, the origin section, and the defining function. The central map is the identity, and both the homeomorphism and its inverse are jointly continuous over the original disk. These are exactly the assertions of Theorem \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemmas \\(2.1\\) and \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking representatives, the spatial critical locus is exactly \\(\\Gamma\\), the morphism \\(X\\to\\Delta\\) is flat, its fibers are normal Gorenstein surfaces, and \\(X\\) is a normal Gorenstein threefold smooth over …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F(x,t)=f_t(x)\\) be a holomorphic family in three spatial variables over a disk \\(\\Delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(f_t(0)=0\\), that \\(0\\) is an isolated critical point of every \\(f_t\\), and that \\(\\mu(f_t,0)=\\mu&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  X=F^{-1}(0),\\qquad X_t=f_t^{-1}(0),\\qquad\n  \\Gamma=\\{0\\}\\times\\Delta.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\chi_c\\) for Euler characteristic with compact supports.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking representatives, the spatial critical locus is exactly \\(\\Gamma\\), the morphism \\(X\\to\\Delta\\) is flat, its fibers are normal Gorenstein surfaces, and \\(X\\) is a normal Gorenstein threefold smooth over \\(\\Delta\\) away from \\(\\Gamma\\). These properties persist after a nonconstant holomorphic base change between smooth disks.</p><p>There are a sufficiently small closed Euclidean ball \\(B\\) centered at \\(0\\), a smaller parameter disk, and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) such that, for every parameter in that disk:\n\\[\n0\\text{ is the only spatial critical point in }B,\n\\]\n\\[\nf_t^{-1}(c)\\text{ meets }\\partial B\\text{ transversely whenever }|c|&lt;\\eta,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\chi(X_t\\cap B)=1,\\qquad\n\\chi_c\\bigl((X_t\\cap B)\\setminus\\{0\\}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nThe Euler equalities also hold when the radius of \\(B\\) varies slightly in a sufficiently small interval about the chosen radius.</p>", "proof": "<p>The local family assertions are Lemma \\(1.1\\). We prove the fixed-ball assertions, originally Lemma \\(2.2\\).</p><p>Choose \\(B\\) inside the representative of Lemma \\(1.1\\), with radius a Milnor radius for \\(f_0\\). Transversality at the compact set\n\\[\nf_0^{-1}(0)\\cap\\partial B\n\\]\npersists for small \\(t\\) and small \\(c\\). By compactness, points elsewhere on the sphere do not enter these small levels after shrinking. This proves the critical-locus and transversality assertions.</p><p>Fix \\(c\\) with \\(0&lt;|c|&lt;\\eta\\). The spaces\n\\[\nf_t^{-1}(c)\\cap B\n\\]\nform a proper smooth family with boundary over the smaller parameter disk. Smooth lifts tangent to the boundary give the proper-submersion theorem in this setting. Their Euler characteristic is that of the central Milnor fiber:\n\\[\n\\chi(f_t^{-1}(c)\\cap B)=1+\\mu\n\\]\nby [22, Theorems 6.5 and 7.2].</p><p>For a fixed \\(t\\), choose an inner Milnor ball for \\(f_t\\) whose closure lies in the interior of \\(B\\). On the closed shell between the two spheres, \\(f_t\\) has no critical point, and both boundary restrictions are submersive over a sufficiently small disk of values. The shell therefore has the same Euler contribution at value \\(0\\) and at sufficiently small nonzero values.</p><p>In the inner ball, passing from value \\(0\\) to a sufficiently small nonzero value replaces a contractible singular fiber by a Milnor fiber of Euler characteristic \\(1+\\mu\\). Additivity of \\(\\chi_c\\), or decomposition using the inner boundary, gives\n\\[\n\\chi(f_t^{-1}(c)\\cap B)-\\chi(X_t\\cap B)=\\mu.\n\\]\nThe inner ball and the admissible nonzero value may depend on \\(t\\), but the outer smooth-level computation applies to every such value. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\chi(X_t\\cap B)=1.\n\\]\nRemoving the origin gives\n\\[\n\\chi_c\\bigl((X_t\\cap B)\\setminus\\{0\\}\\bigr)=0.\n\\]</p><p>Transversality persists for radii in a small interval around the chosen radius, and the identical argument applies there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.1\\), “Local family properties.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are representatives on which the spatial critical locus of \\(F\\) is exactly \\(\\Gamma\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the family, constant Milnor number, and notation of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are representatives on which the spatial critical locus of \\(F\\) is exactly \\(\\Gamma\\). On these representatives \\(X\\to\\Delta\\) is flat, its fibers are normal Gorenstein surfaces, and \\(X\\) is a normal Gorenstein threefold smooth over \\(\\Delta\\) away from \\(\\Gamma\\). The same assertions hold after a nonconstant holomorphic base change between smooth disks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider the scheme defined by the three spatial partial derivatives. Its central fiber is supported at \\(0\\) and has length \\(\\mu\\). The local finiteness theorem for analytic maps gives a finite representative over the disk.</p><p>In the regular local ring\n\\[\n\\mathbb C\\{x_1,x_2,x_3,t\\},\n\\]\nthe three partials together with \\(t\\) form a system of parameters, hence a regular sequence. The quotient by the three partials is Cohen–Macaulay of dimension one, and \\(t\\) is a nonzerodivisor in it. It is therefore finite flat over the disk, of degree \\(\\mu\\). In every nearby fiber its local length at \\(0\\) is already \\(\\mu\\), so there can be no additional points. This is conservation of the spatial-gradient number; compare [5, Proposition 3.1].</p><p>Each surface fiber is a hypersurface, hence Gorenstein and Cohen–Macaulay. Its isolated singularity has codimension two, so Serre’s criterion gives normality. The total hypersurface is likewise Gorenstein and Cohen–Macaulay; it is smooth off \\(\\Gamma\\), which has codimension two in \\(X\\), and is therefore normal.</p><p>No fiber of the ambient projection is contained in \\(X\\). Thus its parameter is a nonzerodivisor on \\(X\\), and torsion-freeness over the regular one-dimensional base gives flatness.</p><p>After a disk base change, the equation is again a relative hypersurface with the same individual surface germs and the same spatial critical locus. The preceding arguments apply again.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemmas \\(2.3\\), \\(3.1\\), \\(3.2\\), \\(4.1\\), \\(4.3\\), and Proposition \\(4.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The invariant \\(\\beta\\) is independent of the good resolution and agrees with its value on the minimal semigood resolution.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a normal surface singularity \\((V,p)\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\rho:\\widetilde V\\to V\n  \\]\n  be a good resolution with full reduced simple-normal-crossing exceptional divisor \\(E=\\sum_jE_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a rational divisor \\(Q\\) on \\(\\widetilde V\\), let \\(\\nu(Q)\\) be the unique rational exceptional cycle satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\nu(Q)\\cdot E_j=Q\\cdot E_j\n  \\quad\\text{for every }j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write the relative Zariski decomposition\n  \\[\n  K_{\\widetilde V}+E=P+N,\n  \\]\n  where \\(N\\) is effective and exceptional, \\(P\\cdot E_j\\ge0\\), and \\(P\\cdot E_j=0\\) whenever \\(E_j\\) occurs in \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define Wahl’s invariant\n  \\[\n  \\beta(V,p)=-\\nu(P)^2=-P_{\\mathrm{loc}}^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\mu&gt;0\\) and \\(N\\ge\\mu+1\\). Let \\(V_N\\) be the affine space of polynomials in three variables of degree at most \\(N\\), with zero constant and linear terms, and let\n  \\[\n  U_\\mu=\\left\\{g\\in V_N:\n  \\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathbb C\\{x\\}/J(g)=\\mu\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Denote by \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\) the following algebraic assertion: for every polynomial family in three variables with regular coefficients on a smooth complex algebraic curve, with zero constant and linear terms and constant positive Milnor number at the origin, the Wahl invariant at a marked non-log-canonical fiber equals its value on nearby general fibers.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The invariant \\(\\beta\\) is independent of the good resolution and agrees with its value on the minimal semigood resolution.</p><p>Every isolated function germ of Milnor number \\(\\mu\\) is analytically right-equivalent to its \\(N\\)-jet. The locus \\(U_\\mu\\) is locally closed algebraic, and \\(\\beta\\) on \\(U_\\mu\\) has finite image and is algebraically constructible.</p><p>If \\(V\\) is an integral normal projective surface, smooth outside \\(p\\), with Cartier canonical divisor, and\n\\[\nb:S\\to V\n\\]\nis projective birational, isomorphic outside \\(p\\), with full reduced inverse image \\(B\\) a divisor, \\((S,B)\\) log canonical, and \\(K_S+B\\) \\(b\\)-nef, then\n\\[\n\\beta(V,p)=K_V^2-(K_S+B)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Log canonicity is constant in a holomorphic \\(\\mu\\)-constant family of isolated hypersurface singularities in three spatial variables over a connected smooth complex curve.</p><p>A bounded-degree polynomial \\(\\mu\\)-constant family over a smooth algebraic curve admits, after shrinking about a marked point, a projective hypersurface compactification of fixed degree \\(d\\) having precisely the prescribed singularity in each fiber and smooth complement of its section. Its fibers satisfy\n\\[\nK_{X_c}^2=d(d-4)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\chi_c(X_c\\setminus\\{\\Gamma(c)\\})\n=d(d^2-4d+6)-\\mu-1.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, assuming \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\), the invariant \\(\\beta(X_t,0)\\) is constant near \\(t=0\\) in every holomorphic \\(\\mu\\)-constant family whose central surface germ is non-log-canonical. Only individual analytic right-equivalences to polynomial germs are required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(2.1\\)–\\(2.5\\) establish the finite-determinacy, intersection, log-canonicity, constructibility, and compactification assertions. It remains to prove the scalar curve reduction, originally Proposition \\(4.2\\).</p><p>Let \\(F(x,t)=f_t(x)\\) be a holomorphic \\(\\mu\\)-constant family with non-log-canonical central surface. Take \\(N\\ge\\mu+1\\) and consider\n\\[\na:\\Delta\\to V_N,\\qquad a(t)=j^Nf_t.\n\\]\nBy Lemma \\(2.1\\), \\(a(t)\\in U_\\mu\\), and this replacement preserves each individual analytic surface germ and its Wahl invariant. Put\n\\[\na_0=a(0),\\qquad \\beta_0=\\beta(V(a_0),0).\n\\]</p><p>If constancy fails on every smaller disk, \\(a_0\\) lies in the Zariski closure of the constructible set\n\\[\nZ_{\\ne}=\\{g\\in U_\\mu:\\beta(V(g),0)\\ne\\beta_0\\}.\n\\]\nIndeed an accumulating sequence of coefficient vectors forces this inclusion.</p><p>Choose an irreducible locally closed subset of \\(Z_{\\ne}\\) whose closure in \\(U_\\mu\\) contains \\(a_0\\). After restricting to an affine neighborhood, its closure is an irreducible affine variety \\(Z\\) of positive dimension, containing \\(a_0\\) and a dense open\n\\[\nZ^\\circ\\subset Z_{\\ne}.\n\\]</p><p>There is an algebraic curve in \\(Z\\) through \\(a_0\\) whose general point lies in \\(Z^\\circ\\). To construct it, take a finite Noether normalization\n\\[\n\\pi:Z\\to\\mathbb A^d\n\\]\nand translate so that \\(\\pi(a_0)=0\\). The image of \\(Z\\setminus Z^\\circ\\) is proper closed. Choose a line through \\(0\\) not contained in that image. Its inverse image is cut out by \\(d-1\\) equations, so each irreducible component through \\(a_0\\) has dimension at least one. Finiteness gives the opposite inequality and prevents such a component from mapping to a point. It therefore dominates the line and has general point in \\(Z^\\circ\\).</p><p>Normalize the component, choose a point over \\(a_0\\), and remove finitely many other points as needed. This gives a marked smooth curve \\(C\\) with regular polynomial coefficients induced from coefficient space.</p><p>Every member lies in \\(U_\\mu\\). The marked surface is non-log-canonical, while its general Wahl invariant differs from \\(\\beta_0\\), contradicting \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\). Thus \\(\\beta\\) is constant in the original analytic family after shrinking.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.3\\), “Individual finite determinacy.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(b\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+2}\\), there is a biholomorphic germ \\(h\\) fixing \\(0\\) such that \\( (f+b)\\circ h=f. \\) In particular, if \\(N\\ge\\mu+1\\), then \\(f\\) is analytically right-equivalent to its Taylor …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\in\\mathbb C\\{x_1,x_2,x_3\\}\\) have an isolated critical point at \\(0\\) and Milnor number \\(\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\mathfrak m=(x_1,x_2,x_3)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(b\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+2}\\), there is a biholomorphic germ \\(h\\) fixing \\(0\\) such that\n\\[\n(f+b)\\circ h=f.\n\\]\nIn particular, if \\(N\\ge\\mu+1\\), then \\(f\\) is analytically right-equivalent to its Taylor polynomial \\(j^Nf\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nJ(f)=(f_{x_1},f_{x_2},f_{x_3}).\n\\]\nThe local algebra \\(\\mathbb C\\{x\\}/J(f)\\) has length \\(\\mu\\), so its maximal ideal has \\(\\mu\\)-th power zero, by the descending filtration and Nakayama’s lemma. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m^\\mu\\subset J(f).\n\\]\nFor \\(b\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+2}\\),\n\\[\nb\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+2}\\subset\\mathfrak m^2J(f),\n\\qquad\nb_{x_i}\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+1}\\subset\\mathfrak mJ(f).\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(b=a^Tg\\), where \\(g=(f_{x_i})_i\\) is a column vector and the entries of \\(a\\) lie in \\(\\mathfrak m^2\\). There is a matrix \\(C\\) with entries in \\(\\mathfrak m\\) such that the gradient column of \\(f+sb\\) is\n\\[\n(I+sC)g.\n\\]\nOn a sufficiently small neighborhood this matrix is invertible simultaneously for \\(0\\le s\\le1\\). The holomorphic spatial vector field\n\\[\nw_s=-(I+sC)^{-T}a\n\\]\nvanishes to second order and satisfies\n\\[\nd(f+sb)(w_s)=-b.\n\\]</p><p>Its time-dependent flow \\(h_s\\), initially the identity, exists as a holomorphic germ throughout \\(0\\le s\\le1\\), after shrinking the spatial neighborhood. This follows from a uniform quadratic bound on \\(w_s\\); the reverse equation supplies the inverse germ. The chain rule gives\n\\[\n\\frac d{ds}\\bigl((f+sb)\\circ h_s\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(h=h_1\\) has the required property. Taking \\(b=j^Nf-f\\) proves the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.1\\), together with the resolution-independence argument immediately preceding it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The value \\(\\beta\\) is unchanged on higher good resolutions and agrees with its value on the minimal semigood resolution.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the relative Zariski decomposition and invariant \\(\\beta\\) defined in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the projective formula, let \\(V\\) be an integral normal projective complex surface, smooth outside \\(p\\), with Cartier canonical divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b:S\\to V\\) be projective birational and isomorphic outside \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that the reduced inverse image of \\(p\\) is a divisor \\(B\\), that \\((S,B)\\) is log canonical, and that \\(K_S+B\\) is \\(b\\)-nef.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The value \\(\\beta\\) is unchanged on higher good resolutions and agrees with its value on the minimal semigood resolution. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\beta(V,p)=K_V^2-(K_S+B)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First let\n\\[\n\\pi:\\widehat V\\to\\widetilde V\n\\]\nbe a further resolution, isomorphic off \\(E\\), with full reduced exceptional divisor \\(\\widehat E\\) over \\(p\\). Log canonicity of the normal-crossings pair gives\n\\[\nK_{\\widehat V}+\\widehat E\n=\\pi^*(K_{\\widetilde V}+E)+G,\n\\qquad G\\ge0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(G\\) is \\(\\pi\\)-exceptional. Its relative decomposition is\n\\[\n\\pi^*P+(\\pi^*N+G):\n\\]\nthe first summand is relatively nef and is orthogonal to every component of the second. Uniqueness of the relative decomposition applies. Checking intersections against strict transforms and \\(\\pi\\)-exceptional curves gives\n\\[\n\\nu(\\pi^*P)=\\pi^*\\nu(P).\n\\]\nThe square is unchanged. A common higher resolution compares any two choices, including a semigood resolution.</p><p>For the projective formula, choose a log resolution\n\\[\n\\pi:Z\\to S\n\\]\nisomorphic outside \\(B\\), and put \\(\\rho=b\\pi\\). Let \\(E\\) be the full reduced exceptional divisor of \\(\\rho\\). With compatible canonical divisors, log canonicity gives\n\\[\nK_Z+E=\\pi^*(K_S+B)+N,\n\\]\nwhere \\(N\\) is effective and \\(\\pi\\)-exceptional. The pullback on the right is \\(\\rho\\)-nef and has intersection zero with every component of \\(N\\). It is therefore the positive part \\(P\\) of the relative decomposition.</p><p>The divisor\n\\[\nZ_P=P-\\rho^*K_V\n\\]\nis exceptional, because its two summands agree outside \\(\\rho^{-1}(p)\\). Since \\(\\rho^*K_V\\) has intersection zero with every exceptional curve,\n\\[\nZ_P=\\nu(P).\n\\]\nThe projection formula gives\n\\[\nP^2=K_V^2+Z_P^2=(K_S+B)^2.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(\\beta=-\\nu(P)^2\\), this proves the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.2\\), “Persistence of log canonicity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Log canonicity of the normal surface germ is constant throughout the family.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a holomorphic family of isolated hypersurface singularities in three spatial variables over a connected smooth complex curve.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume its Milnor number is constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Log canonicity of the normal surface germ is constant throughout the family.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Varchenko’s theorem [31], as recovered by Steenbrink, the spectrum of an isolated hypersurface singularity is constant in a \\(\\mu\\)-constant deformation [27, Theorem 2.8]. Its smallest number plus one is the complex singularity index [27, Theorem 2.11]. The log canonical threshold is the minimum of this index and one [15, Theorem 9.5].</p><p>For a normal hypersurface in a smooth ambient space, adjunction and inversion of adjunction identify log canonicity with threshold one [12]. To apply the algebraic inversion theorem to an individual analytic germ, replace its defining function by a sufficiently high Taylor polynomial using Lemma \\(2.1\\). Analytic right-equivalence preserves both the threshold and log canonicity.</p><p>Thus log canonicity, and its failure, is constant. This use of the spectrum concerns the individual isolated function germs and does not assume topological triviality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.1\\), “The precise jet locus.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The subset \\(U_\\mu\\) is locally closed algebraic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\mu&gt;0\\), \\(N\\ge\\mu+1\\), and the spaces \\(V_N,U_\\mu\\) of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The subset \\(U_\\mu\\) is locally closed algebraic. On \\(U_\\mu\\), the function\n\\[\ng\\longmapsto\\beta(V(g),0)\n\\]\nhas finite image and is algebraically constructible.</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in the finite-dimensional algebra\n\\[\nA_\\mu=\\mathbb C[x_1,x_2,x_3]/\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+1}.\n\\]\nThe ideal generated by the partials of \\(g\\) is the image of a linear map\n\\[\nA_\\mu^{\\oplus3}\\to A_\\mu\n\\]\nwhose matrix entries depend polynomially on the coefficients of \\(g\\). Requiring its cokernel to have dimension \\(\\mu\\) is a locally closed rank condition.</p><p>This tests the full local length, including exclusion of infinite length. Indeed, let\n\\[\nA=\\mathbb C\\{x\\}/J(g)\n\\]\nwith maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak n\\), and suppose\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}A/\\mathfrak n^{\\mu+1}=\\mu.\n\\]\nThe \\(\\mu+1\\) layers\n\\[\n\\mathfrak n^j/\\mathfrak n^{j+1},\n\\qquad 0\\le j\\le\\mu,\n\\]\ncannot all be nonzero. If one vanishes, Nakayama’s lemma applied to the finitely generated ideal \\(\\mathfrak n^j\\) gives \\(\\mathfrak n^j=0\\). Thus \\(A\\) already has length \\(\\mu\\). The converse follows from the same filtration. Polynomial, localized algebraic, and convergent-power-series calculations give the same truncated quotient.</p><p>For constructibility, let \\(B\\) be an irreducible locally closed subvariety of \\(U_\\mu\\), and replace it by a smooth dense open. Consider the universal hypersurface with the origin section. After another dense-open restriction, there is a Zariski neighborhood of this section on which the relative singular locus is exactly the section.</p><p>To see this, isolatedness in every fiber makes the section an irreducible component of the relative critical locus: a larger component containing it would have a positive-dimensional fiber at a general section point, by the dimension inequality. Other components meet the section over proper closed subsets of \\(B\\). Remove those subsets, then remove the other components from the total space. The resulting total hypersurface is Cohen–Macaulay and regular off a codimension-two subset, hence normal. Its fibers have dimension two, so miracle flatness over the smooth base gives flatness; it is smooth over \\(B\\) off the section.</p><p>Take a projective resolution, isomorphic off the section, principalizing its ideal and having simple-normal-crossing reduced exceptional divisor \\(E\\), using [33, Theorems 1.0.1–1.0.3]. Generic smoothness for the resolution space and every intersection of components of \\(E\\) permits a further dense-open restriction such that the resolution is smooth over \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) is relative simple normal crossing. Discard nondominating exceptional strata. Because the resolution is projective and \\(E\\) lies over the section, \\(E\\) and all its component intersections are proper over \\(B\\).</p><p>The fibers are good resolutions of the surface germs. Their weighted exceptional dual graphs are locally constant in the complex topology, up to permutation of vertices. Smooth proper variation preserves connected components and genera of exceptional curves, their intersection points, and incidence. Self-intersections are degrees of normal line bundles obtained from divisor line bundles on the resolution space; those degrees are locally constant in smooth proper curve families. If a total divisor has several fiber components, they are disjoint because that relative divisor is smooth, and the degree calculation applies separately.</p><p>The weighted graph determines the exceptional intersection matrix. Adjunction gives\n\\[\nK\\cdot E_i=2g(E_i)-2-E_i^2,\n\\]\nso the graph determines all intersections of \\(K+E\\), its relative Zariski decomposition, and the square of its numerical exceptional representative. Hence it determines \\(\\beta\\).</p><p>An irreducible smooth complex base is connected, so \\(\\beta\\) is constant on this dense open. Noetherian induction on the remaining closed subsets yields a finite stratification of \\(U_\\mu\\) on which \\(\\beta\\) is constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.3\\), “Compactification with one singular point.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After replacing \\(C\\) by a Zariski neighborhood of \\(0\\), there is a flat projective hypersurface family \\( X\\subset\\mathbb P^3\\times C \\) of fixed degree \\(d\\), with section \\(\\Gamma\\), such that each fiber is integral …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a smooth connected complex algebraic curve with marked point \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_c(x_1,x_2,x_3)\\) be polynomials of bounded degree \\(l\\), with coefficients regular on \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  g_c(0)=0,\\qquad d_xg_c(0)=0,\n  \\]\n  and that the origin is an isolated critical point of constant finite Milnor number \\(\\mu\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After replacing \\(C\\) by a Zariski neighborhood of \\(0\\), there is a flat projective hypersurface family\n\\[\nX\\subset\\mathbb P^3\\times C\n\\]\nof fixed degree \\(d\\), with section \\(\\Gamma\\), such that each fiber is integral and normal, smooth outside \\(\\Gamma(c)\\), and its germ there has a defining function analytically right-equivalent to \\(g_c\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nK_{X_c}^2=d(d-4)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\chi_c(X_c\\setminus\\{\\Gamma(c)\\})\n=d(d^2-4d+6)-\\mu-1.\n\\]\nThe total space is normal and Gorenstein. These conclusions persist after a finite change of smooth base curves.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose\n\\[\nd&gt;l,\\qquad d\\ge\\mu+2,\n\\]\nand homogeneous coordinates \\([x_1:x_2:x_3:w]\\) on \\(\\mathbb P^3\\). Homogenize \\(g_c\\) to degree \\(d\\), obtaining \\(G_c(x,w)\\), and set\n\\[\nX_c=\\{G_c(x,w)+Q_d(x)=0\\},\n\\qquad\n\\Gamma(c)=[0:0:0:1],\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q_d\\) is a general homogeneous form of degree \\(d\\) in the spatial coordinates, independent of \\(c\\).</p><p>Since \\(Q_d\\in\\mathfrak m^{\\mu+2}\\), Lemma \\(2.1\\) shows separately for each \\(c\\) that adding \\(Q_d\\) preserves the analytic right type. This is the high-degree-tail construction of [27, Lemma 2.5].</p><p>At \\(c=0\\), the linear system spanned by \\(G_0\\) and all forms \\(Q_d(x)\\) has base locus exactly \\([0:0:0:1]\\). Bertini’s theorem [14, Theorem 4.1] makes a general member smooth elsewhere, and its nonzero coefficient of \\(G_0\\) can be normalized to one.</p><p>Equivalently, on\n\\[\nU=\\mathbb P^3\\setminus\\{[0:0:0:1]\\},\n\\]\nthe incidence variety\n\\[\n\\{(z,Q):G_0(z)+Q(z)=0\\}\n\\subset U\\times H^0(\\mathbb P^2,\\mathcal O(d))\n\\]\nis an affine bundle over \\(U\\), since evaluation of the forms is surjective at every \\(z\\in U\\). It is smooth. Its generic fiber over coefficient space is regular by localization, hence smooth over its characteristic-zero function field. The nonsmooth locus has constructible image missing the generic point, so a nonempty open set of choices has smooth fibers.</p><p>There are no additional singularities for complex parameters sufficiently near \\(0\\). Otherwise projectivity produces a sequence of singular points converging to the unique singular point of \\(X_0\\). They eventually lie in \\(w=1\\), where conservation of the spatial-gradient number contradicts their existence: the origin already accounts for the full number \\(\\mu\\). Parameters with an additional singular point form a constructible subset of \\(C\\). Since this subset misses an analytic neighborhood of \\(0\\), it is finite; remove it.</p><p>Every resulting projective hypersurface has only one singular point. It is reduced and irreducible, since a repeated component or an intersection of distinct surface components would give a positive-dimensional singular locus. It is Cohen–Macaulay and regular in codimension one, hence normal. Its equation is nonzero on every ambient fiber, so it is a relative effective Cartier divisor and the family is flat. It is smooth over \\(C\\) outside the section. The total hypersurface is Gorenstein and regular in codimension one, hence normal.</p><p>Adjunction gives\n\\[\n\\omega_{X_c}=\\mathcal O_{X_c}(d-4),\n\\]\nand therefore \\(K_{X_c}^2=d(d-4)^2\\).</p><p>A small global smoothing changes only a Milnor neighborhood: outside it the family is a proper submersion with transverse boundary. Replacing the contractible singular neighborhood by its Milnor fiber increases Euler characteristic by \\(\\mu\\). A smooth degree-\\(d\\) surface in \\(\\mathbb P^3\\) has Euler characteristic\n\\[\nd(d^2-4d+6),\n\\]\nby the tangent-normal exact sequence and Gauss–Bonnet. Subtracting \\(\\mu\\), then the section point, gives the stated formula. A smoothing may be obtained by subtracting \\(\\varepsilon w^d\\); locally it is the Milnor smoothing, and compactness preserves smoothness elsewhere.</p><p>A base change leaves individual projective surfaces and germs unchanged. Over a smooth new curve, the relative hypersurface description, flatness, smoothness off the section, and the codimension-one argument apply again.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.1\\), “The relative log model.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking \\(C\\) about \\(0\\) and making a finite base change from a smooth curve, there is a projective birational morphism \\( h:Y\\to X. \\) Write \\(X,C,\\Gamma,0\\) for the base-changed objects, and let \\(H\\) be the …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a smooth connected complex algebraic curve with marked point \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:X\\to C\\) be a flat projective family of integral normal Gorenstein surfaces.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X\\) is normal and Gorenstein, a section \\(\\Gamma\\subset X\\) contains every fiber singularity, and \\(\\pi\\) is smooth on \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A pair is log canonical if all log discrepancies are nonnegative. A pair with boundary coefficients at most one is divisorial log terminal, abbreviated dlt, if it has a log resolution on which every exceptional divisor has positive log discrepancy.</p><p class=\"li\">A divisor is nef over a morphism if it has nonnegative degree on every complete contracted curve.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking \\(C\\) about \\(0\\) and making a finite base change from a smooth curve, there is a projective birational morphism\n\\[\nh:Y\\to X.\n\\]\nWrite \\(X,C,\\Gamma,0\\) for the base-changed objects, and let \\(H\\) be the reduced sum of all \\(h\\)-exceptional prime divisors dominating \\(C\\). Then:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(Y\\) is normal and \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial, and \\(h\\) is an isomorphism over \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every scheme fiber \\(Y_c\\) is reduced Cartier, and \\((Y,H+Y_c)\\) is dlt.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(K_Y+H\\) is nef over \\(X\\). Every vertical \\(h\\)-exceptional prime divisor lies in \\(Y_0\\) and maps to \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For \\(c\\ne0\\), \\(Y_c\\) is integral and normal, and \\(H|_{Y_c}\\) is reduced as a Weil divisor.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. The fiber \\(Y_0\\) has a unique component dominating \\(X_0\\); all other components are projective surfaces over \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_0\\).</p><p>This assertion identifies the full surviving horizontal divisorial boundary. It does not yet identify the entire set \\(h^{-1}(\\Gamma)\\) with the boundary or determine the different on every fiber component.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a projective log resolution\n\\[\ng:Z\\to X\n\\]\nprincipalizing the ideal of \\(\\Gamma\\) and resolving its union with \\(X_0\\). Choose it to be an isomorphism over \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\), where the total space and special fiber are already smooth. Thus \\(Z\\) is smooth, \\(g^{-1}(\\Gamma)\\) has divisorial support, and its union with the support of \\(Z_0\\) is simple normal crossing. Every exceptional divisor lies over \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p>Generic smoothness for \\(Z\\to C\\) and the finitely many exceptional strata permits deletion of finitely many base points other than \\(0\\), so that \\(Z\\to C\\) is smooth away from \\(0\\) and every fiber support together with the exceptional divisor is simple normal crossing. Every vertical exceptional divisor then lies over \\(0\\).</p><p>Let \\(H_Z\\) be the reduced horizontal exceptional divisor and write\n\\[\nZ_0=\\sum_j a_jZ_j.\n\\]\nChoose \\(b\\) divisible by all \\(a_j\\), and a regular parameter \\(\\tau\\) with no other zero. The cover \\(s^b=\\tau\\), with smooth normalization, is finite, ramified to order \\(b\\) over \\(0\\), and étale elsewhere.</p><p>The pulled-back target remains normal and Gorenstein. Locally it is\n\\[\nA[s]/(s^b-\\tau)\n\\]\nover a Gorenstein ring \\(A\\), so it is Gorenstein and satisfies \\(S_2\\). Off the section it is smooth over the new smooth curve; the section has codimension two. It therefore satisfies \\(R_1\\) and is normal. Its fibers remain integral, so the flat total space over the connected covering curve is integral.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(3.1\\) with marking \\(H_Z\\). It gives a smooth \\(V\\), projective over the normalized pullback of \\(Z\\), with reduced simple-normal-crossing special fiber together with the transformed horizontal marking. It is an isomorphism away from \\(0\\), and also over \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\). Thus every exceptional divisor still lies over \\(\\Gamma\\), and every vertical exceptional divisor lies over \\(0\\).</p><p>Let \\(H_V\\) be the reduced sum of horizontal exceptional primes. It is precisely the transformed marking. For every \\(c\\),\n\\[\n(V,H_V+V_c)\n\\]\nis simple normal crossing; hence \\((V,H_V)\\to C\\) is a dlt morphism in the sense of [17, Definition 7.1].</p><p>Apply the relative semistable minimal model theorem [17, Theorem 7.9] to \\((V,H_V)\\) over \\(X\\). Its contractions and flips are projective over \\(X\\), it terminates, and the resulting \\((Y,H)\\to C\\) remains dlt. A Mori fiber space cannot occur, because it would factor the dominant map from the threefold to \\(X\\) through a variety of dimension less than three. Therefore\n\\[\nK_Y+H\n\\]\nis nef over \\(X\\).</p><p>The program extracts no divisors. Every surviving horizontal exceptional prime retains coefficient one, and every vertical exceptional prime remains over \\(0\\). Every step is an isomorphism over \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\), since there are no relative curves there to contract. Preservation of \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factoriality gives the asserted model.</p><p>For the scheme fibers, a pulled-back local parameter \\(t\\) is a nonzerodivisor on the integral normal \\(Y\\), so \\(Y_c\\) is Cartier. Its order along every prime component is one: the corresponding valuation occurred on \\(V\\), where the fiber was reduced, and no divisors were extracted. Such a principal divisor defines a reduced subscheme. Indeed, if \\(u^n\\in(t)\\), height-one valuations imply that \\(u/t\\) is regular at every height-one prime, and normality gives \\(u/t\\in\\mathcal O_Y\\). Hence \\((t)\\) is radical.</p><p>For \\(c\\ne0\\), no vertical exceptional divisors occur, and the integral \\(X_c\\) has only its strict transform as a component of \\(Y_c\\). Dlt gives normality. At the generic point of each curve in \\(H\\cap Y_c\\), the pair is simple normal crossing, so the Cartier fiber cuts \\(H\\) with multiplicity one.</p><p>Over \\(0\\), there is exactly one strict transform of \\(X_0\\); every other component is exceptional, maps to \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_0\\), and is projective because \\(h\\) is projective.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(A.3\\), “Marked monomial reduction in dimension three.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After the finite base change \\(s^b=\\tau\\) and normalization, there is a projective birational modification whose total space is smooth and whose special fiber is reduced and simple normal crossing together with the …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q:Z\\to C\\) be a morphism from a smooth quasi-projective complex threefold to a smooth curve.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  Z_0=\\sum_i a_iZ_i\n  \\]\n  be the fiber over \\(0\\in C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a reduced divisor with horizontal components.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((Z_0)_{\\mathrm{red}}+H\\) is simple normal crossing, and away from \\(0\\), \\(q\\) is smooth with relative simple-normal-crossing divisor \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">After shrinking about \\(0\\), choose a parameter \\(\\tau\\) with no other zero and an integer \\(b\\) divisible by every \\(a_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After the finite base change \\(s^b=\\tau\\) and normalization, there is a projective birational modification whose total space is smooth and whose special fiber is reduced and simple normal crossing together with the transformed horizontal marking. The modification is an isomorphism on every open set where the original morphism was smooth and the marking relatively simple normal crossing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Work componentwise if the normalized base change is disconnected. At a point of \\(Z_0\\), choose simple-normal-crossing coordinates in which\n\\[\n\\tau=u\\prod_{i\\in I}z_i^{a_i},\n\\qquad u\\text{ a unit},\\qquad |I|\\le3.\n\\]\nHorizontal components present are among the remaining coordinate hyperplanes. An étale root of \\(u\\) reduces the calculation to the monomial equation. Each normalized local sheet is toric in the vertical coordinates, with\n\\[\n\\sigma_I=\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{I},\n\\qquad\nN_I=\n\\left\\{(d_i)\\in\\mathbb Z^I:\n\\sum_{i\\in I}a_id_i\\equiv0\\pmod b\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe order of \\(s\\) is the integral height\n\\[\n\\ell(d)=\\frac{\\sum_i a_id_i}{b}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a_i\\mid b\\), the primitive generator \\((b/a_i)e_i\\) of each original ray has height one. Other coordinates, including horizontal markings, are unchanged product factors.</p><p>Subdivide by inserting all height-one lattice rays. There are finitely many, since their intersections with \\(\\ell=1\\) lie in compact simplices. Insert each point not already a ray by a star subdivision of its smallest containing cone. Starting with simplicial cones keeps every cone simplicial.</p><p>For compatibility, carry out the sequence on the single orthant with one coordinate for every global \\(Z_i\\), using the congruence lattice with the same \\(a_i\\), and restrict to faces that occur. A point not on a face does not change that face.</p><p>These subdivisions have a global projective realization. A vertical ray \\(v\\) records the orders of the pullbacks of the original \\(Z_i\\). Let \\(G_v\\) be the sum of all vertical prime divisors having that order vector. Simpliciality makes \\(G_v\\) rational Cartier. If the next ray is interior to the cone generated by \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_k\\), write\n\\[\nv=\\sum_j\\lambda_jv_j,\\qquad \\lambda_j&gt;0.\n\\]\nChoose positive integers \\(c_j\\) such that \\(c_jG_{v_j}\\) are Cartier and all \\(c_j\\lambda_j\\) are equal. Normalize the blowup of\n\\[\n\\mathcal I=\\sum_j\\mathcal O(-c_jG_{v_j}).\n\\]\nOn toric charts its generators are monomials. The regions where one order is smallest give the star subdivision at \\(v\\), with equality\n\\[\nc_1d_1=\\cdots=c_kd_k\n\\]\nin ray coordinates. On a chart missing a required ray, the corresponding summand is the unit ideal, so the modification is the identity. Thus the normalized blowups realize the compatible subdivisions globally, projectively, and preserve horizontal product coordinates.</p><p>In an actual chart, the vertical cone has dimension at most three. Its height-one section is a point, segment, or triangle with no lattice points except vertices. An empty segment is primitive. An empty lattice triangle is unimodular: translate one vertex to zero and let \\(v,w\\) be its edge vectors. If their lattice index exceeded one, a nonzero lattice point in their half-open parallelogram would be\n\\[\n\\alpha v+\\gamma w,\\qquad 0\\le\\alpha,\\gamma&lt;1.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\alpha+\\gamma\\le1\\), this lies in the triangle and is not a vertex. Otherwise\n\\[\n(1-\\alpha)v+(1-\\gamma)w\n\\]\ndoes so. Both contradict emptiness. Hence the edge vectors form a basis of the height-zero lattice; adjoining one height-one vertex gives a basis of the full lattice. The cone is regular. The segment and point cases give the same conclusion in lower dimensions.</p><p>The resulting toric varieties are smooth. Every vertical ray has height one, so \\(\\operatorname{div}(s)\\) is reduced. Regular toric coordinates show that it and the horizontal coordinate divisors are simple normal crossing. Global labels and order vectors distinguish local boundary branches, so distinct branches of one component are not identified.</p><p>Outside the special fiber the cover is étale and no subdivision acts. At a smooth point of the original morphism over \\(0\\), there is one vertical coordinate of multiplicity one, whose cone needs no subdivision. This gives the stated isomorphism property.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.2\\) and Proposition \\(5.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(S\\) is normal, its different is exactly \\(B_S\\), and \\( (K_Y+H+Y_c)|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S, \\qquad L|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S. \\) The pair \\((S,B_S)\\) is dlt.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma:X\\to C\\) be a flat family of normal Gorenstein surfaces over a smooth connected complex curve with distinguished point \\(0\\), and let \\(\\Gamma\\subset X\\) be a section.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X\\) is normal and Gorenstein.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h:Y\\to X\\) be projective birational, with \\(Y\\) normal and \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial, and \\(h\\) an isomorphism over \\(X\\setminus\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be the reduced sum of horizontal \\(h\\)-exceptional prime divisors. Assume all vertical exceptional primes lie over \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume every fiber is reduced Cartier,\n  \\[\n  Y_0=S_0+\\sum_aS_a,\n  \\]\n  where \\(S_0\\to X_0\\) is birational and each \\(S_a\\) maps to \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_0\\); assume \\(Y_c\\) is irreducible for \\(c\\ne0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((Y,H+Y_c)\\) is dlt for every relevant \\(c\\), and\n  \\[\n  L=K_Y+H\n  \\]\n  is \\(h\\)-nef.</p><p class=\"li\">With compatible canonical divisors, put\n  \\[\n  A=L-h^*K_X.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a component \\(S\\subset Y_c\\), let \\(B_S\\) be the reduced union of curves where \\(S\\) meets other components of \\(H+Y_c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X_c\\) is non-log-canonical at \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_c\\) for general \\(c\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(S\\) is normal, its different is exactly \\(B_S\\), and\n\\[\n(K_Y+H+Y_c)|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S,\n\\qquad\nL|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S.\n\\]\nThe pair \\((S,B_S)\\) is dlt. The adjoint divisor is nef on curves contracted by \\(h|_S\\), and is nef on the projective surface \\(S_a\\) for every extra vertical component.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nA\\le0,\n\\]\nsome horizontal coefficient of \\(A\\) is strictly negative, and\n\\[\nh^{-1}(\\Gamma)\n=\\operatorname{Supp}(-A)\n=\\operatorname{Supp}(H)\\cup\\bigcup_aS_a.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(B_{S_0}\\) and \\(B_{Y_c}\\), for \\(c\\ne0\\), are the full reduced exceptional inverse images of the corresponding section points, and\n\\[\nS_0\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}(B_{S_0})\\simeq X_0\\setminus\\Gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\nY_c\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}(B_{Y_c})\\simeq X_c\\setminus\\Gamma.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The adjunction assertions are Lemma \\(4.1\\). We prove the support assertion, originally Proposition \\(5.3\\).</p><p>The divisor\n\\[\nA=K_Y+H-h^*K_X\n\\]\nis exceptional because \\(h\\) is an isomorphism away from \\(\\Gamma\\). It is \\(h\\)-nef because pullbacks from \\(X\\) have degree zero on contracted curves. Apply the negativity lemma to \\(-A\\): its pushforward is zero, so\n\\[\n-A\\ge0\n\\]\nby [8, Lemma 4.16(1)].</p><p>Suppose all horizontal coefficients of \\(A\\) were zero. Its support would lie over \\(0\\), so for general \\(c\\ne0\\), its restriction to \\(Y_c\\) would vanish. Lemma \\(4.1\\) and Cartier-fiber adjunction on \\(X\\) give, with compatible canonical divisors,\n\\[\nK_{Y_c}+B_{Y_c}=h_c^*K_{X_c},\n\\qquad h_c=h|_{Y_c}.\n\\]\nThe pair on the left is log canonical. On a common resolution, discrepancies of divisors exceptional over \\(Y_c\\) are unchanged, while a boundary component on \\(Y_c\\) has discrepancy \\(-1\\) over \\(X_c\\). Thus \\(X_c\\) would be log canonical, a contradiction.</p><p>Hence some horizontal exceptional prime has strictly negative coefficient. Its image is closed by properness, lies in \\(\\Gamma\\), and dominates \\(C\\), so it maps onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Every fiber of \\(h\\) over \\(\\Gamma\\) meets \\(\\operatorname{Supp}(-A)\\).</p><p>The support statement of the negativity lemma says that every fiber of a proper birational morphism between normal varieties either lies in the support of an effective anti-nef divisor or is disjoint from it [8, Lemma 4.16(2)]. Therefore\n\\[\nh^{-1}(\\Gamma)\\subset\\operatorname{Supp}(-A).\n\\]\nThe reverse inclusion follows from exceptionality over \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p>Every prime in this support is a component of \\(H\\) or one of the \\(S_a\\). Conversely all those divisors map into \\(\\Gamma\\), so all lie in the support. This proves the asserted equality.</p><p>Intersect with \\(S_0\\). None of the listed divisors is \\(S_0\\), and their intersections are exactly the boundary curves defining \\(B_{S_0}\\); dlt strata have the expected codimension. There are no additional exceptional points or curves outside this boundary. The same argument on \\(Y_c\\), \\(c\\ne0\\), uses \\(H\\) alone. The complement isomorphisms follow.</p><p>This identifies a divisor on each surface. It does not assert that further adjunction to a boundary curve has zero fractional different, or that the threefold is smooth at every closed boundary point.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.2\\), “Surface adjunction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The component \\(S\\) is normal, and \\( \\operatorname{Diff}_S(H+Y_c-S)=B_S. \\) Thus \\((S,B_S)\\) is dlt and \\( L|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S. \\) This divisor is nef on every curve contracted by \\(h|_S\\); for each extra …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the setting of Proposition \\(4\\), but omit its non-log-canonicity assumption.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be any component of \\(Y_c\\), and let \\(B_S\\) be its reduced boundary as defined there.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The component \\(S\\) is normal, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Diff}_S(H+Y_c-S)=B_S.\n\\]\nThus \\((S,B_S)\\) is dlt and\n\\[\nL|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S.\n\\]\nThis divisor is nef on every curve contracted by \\(h|_S\\); for each extra vertical component \\(S_a\\), it is nef on the projective surface \\(S_a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Dlt adjunction gives normality of \\(S\\) and a dlt pair\n\\[\n\\bigl(S,\\operatorname{Diff}_S(H+Y_c-S)\\bigr)\n\\]\nby [8, Lemma 10.2]. Check the different at every prime curve of \\(S\\).</p><p>If the curve lies in another component of \\(H+Y_c\\), its generic point is an lc stratum. The dlt pair is simple normal crossing there, so ordinary adjunction gives coefficient one.</p><p>At the generic point \\(\\eta\\) of any other prime curve, no other boundary component occurs. Thus \\(S\\) equals the Cartier divisor \\(Y_c\\) near \\(\\eta\\). Since \\(S\\) is normal, \\(\\mathcal O_{S,\\eta}\\) is regular of dimension one. If \\(t\\) defines the fiber and\n\\[\nR=\\mathcal O_{Y,\\eta},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nR/(t)=\\mathcal O_{S,\\eta}.\n\\]\nThe maximal ideal of \\(R\\) is generated by \\(t\\) and one lift of a generator downstairs. Hence its embedding dimension is at most two, and \\(R\\) is regular. Ordinary adjunction gives coefficient zero. This proves equality of the different and \\(B_S\\).</p><p>The line bundle of \\(Y_c=q^*(c)\\), where \\(q=\\sigma h\\), restricts trivially to \\(S\\). This gives the formula for \\(L|_S\\). Its degree on a contracted curve is the degree of \\(L\\), hence nonnegative.</p><p>Each \\(S_a\\) is a closed subvariety of the projective fiber of \\(h\\) over \\(\\Gamma\\cap X_0\\). It is projective, and all its curves are contracted by \\(h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemmas \\(6.1\\), \\(6.2\\), \\(6.4\\), and Proposition \\(6.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A local smoothing of a normal isolated surface singularity has Milnor fiber \\(M\\) satisfying \\( \\chi(M)\\ge1. \\) At a smooth point of a reduced Cartier central surface in a normal threefold, the local nearby Euler …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q:Y\\to C\\) be projective flat from a normal complex threefold to a smooth pointed curve \\((C,0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a reduced divisor with horizontal prime components.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the Cartier fiber\n  \\[\n  Y_0=\\sum_{S\\subset Y_0}S\n  \\]\n  is reduced, \\((Y,Y_0+H)\\) is dlt, and every boundary component is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(Y_t\\setminus H\\) is smooth for all sufficiently small \\(t\\ne0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each component \\(S\\), let \\(B_S\\) be the reduced union of its intersections with other components of \\(Y_0+H\\), and put \\(S^\\circ=S\\setminus B_S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a regular function \\(f\\), let \\(\\psi_f\\) be the unshifted nearby-cycle operation on constructible Euler functions. It depends only on restriction off \\(f^{-1}(0)\\) and commutes with proper pushforward.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A local smoothing of a normal isolated surface singularity has Milnor fiber \\(M\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\chi(M)\\ge1.\n\\]\nAt a smooth point of a reduced Cartier central surface in a normal threefold, the local nearby Euler characteristic is \\(1\\).</p><p>The nearby Euler function\n\\[\n\\psi_q(1_{Y\\setminus H})\n\\]\nvanishes at every point of \\(Y_0\\) lying on at least two components of \\(Y_0+H\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\chi_c(Y_t\\setminus H)\n=\\sum_{S\\subset Y_0}\\chi_c(S^\\circ)+\\delta,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\delta=\n\\sum_{S\\subset Y_0}\n\\sum_{p\\in\\operatorname{Sing}(S)\\cap S^\\circ}\n\\bigl(\\chi(M_p)-1\\bigr)\\ge0.\n\\]\nHere \\(M_p\\) is the local Milnor fiber of the smoothing at \\(p\\).</p><p>In the original formulation of Proposition \\(6.3\\), the comparison is conditional on \\(\\chi(M_p)\\ge1\\); the interior smoothing assertion supplies that condition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\psi_q(1_{Y\\setminus H})\n\\]\nusing a coordinate on \\(C\\) at \\(0\\). Proper compatibility gives\n\\[\n\\chi_c(Y_t\\setminus H)=\\int_{Y_0}\\lambda\\,d\\chi_c.\n\\]</p><p>At points on at least two components of \\(Y_0+H\\), Lemma \\(5.2\\) gives \\(\\lambda=0\\). Every remaining point lies in exactly one \\(S^\\circ\\). Dlt adjunction makes \\(S\\) normal, so its singular points form a finite set.</p><p>At a smooth point of \\(S^\\circ\\), the local reduced Cartier fiber is \\(S\\) alone. Its regular local ring is the ambient local ring modulo the base parameter. The ambient ring is therefore regular, the parameter is a regular coordinate, and \\(\\lambda=1\\).</p><p>At a singular point \\(p\\in S^\\circ\\), a neighborhood misses \\(H\\) and all other special-fiber components. Flatness and smoothness of nearby open fibers give a smoothing of \\((S,p)\\). If \\(M_p\\) is its Milnor fiber, then\n\\[\n\\lambda(p)=\\chi(M_p)\\ge1\n\\]\nby Lemma \\(5.3\\).</p><p>Integration over the disjoint open surfaces and these finitely many corrections gives\n\\[\n\\chi_c(Y_t\\setminus H)\n=\n\\sum_{S\\subset Y_0}\\chi_c(S^\\circ)\n+\n\\sum_{S\\subset Y_0}\n\\sum_{p\\in\\operatorname{Sing}(S)\\cap S^\\circ}\n(\\chi(M_p)-1).\n\\]\nThe second sum is nonnegative.</p><p>The boundary calculation does not require the different obtained by further adjunction to a curve to have integral coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.1\\), “Boundary covers in dimension three.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After replacing \\(Z\\) by a neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(z\\), there is a finite surjective map \\( \\rho:\\widetilde U\\to U \\) from a normal variety such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be a normal complex algebraic threefold.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  B=\\sum_{j=1}^nB_j\n  \\]\n  be reduced with distinct prime components.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((Z,B)\\) is dlt and each \\(B_j\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z\\) lie on at least two \\(B_j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After replacing \\(Z\\) by a neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(z\\), there is a finite surjective map\n\\[\n\\rho:\\widetilde U\\to U\n\\]\nfrom a normal variety such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\rho\\) is étale in codimension one and over \\(U\\setminus B\\), and every total pullback \\(\\rho^*B_j\\) is reduced Cartier.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\((\\widetilde U,\\rho^*B)\\) is dlt and simple normal crossing at every point of \\(\\rho^{-1}(z)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Shrink \\(U\\) so only boundary components through \\(z\\) remain. Choose regular functions \\(g_j\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(g_j)=m_jB_j,\n\\qquad m_j&gt;0.\n\\]\nNormalize \\(U\\) in a field extension obtained by adjoining roots\n\\[\na_j^{m_j}=g_j\n\\]\nfor all \\(j\\). Normalization is finite; choosing one field extension gives a finite surjective map of degree \\(d\\). This is the local boundary-Cartier cover; see [16, Proposition 11.25].</p><p>At a codimension-one point, each \\(g_j\\) is a unit times the \\(m_j\\)-th power of a local equation, or a unit if \\(B_j\\) is absent. Adjoining its root is unramified there. The same argument makes the cover étale wherever all \\(B_j\\) are Cartier, in particular over \\(U\\setminus B\\) and over the simple-normal-crossing locus. Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(a_j)=\\rho^*B_j\n\\]\nhas coefficient one on every supporting prime and is reduced Cartier.</p><p>Put \\(\\widetilde B=\\rho^*B\\). The codimension-one ramification formula gives\n\\[\nK_{\\widetilde U}+\\widetilde B=\\rho^*(K_U+B).\n\\]\nIf a divisorial valuation \\(F\\) upstairs restricts to \\(e\\) times a normalized divisorial valuation \\(E\\) downstairs, then\n\\[\nA(F,\\widetilde U,\\widetilde B)=eA(E,U,B).\n\\]\nIndeed, normalize a model carrying \\(E\\) in the upper function field. At the generic point of \\(F\\), the ramification coefficient is \\(e-1\\); hence discrepancy coefficients satisfy\n\\[\na_F=ea_E+(e-1).\n\\]\nRestriction through a finite extension remains divisorial, because the value group has finite index and the residue field has unchanged transcendence degree. This is [17, Proposition 5.20].</p><p>The upper pair is therefore log canonical, and every upper lc center maps onto a lower lc center. Every lower center meets the simple-normal-crossing locus because the lower pair is dlt, and the cover is étale there. Every upper center thus meets the simple-normal-crossing locus, giving the dlt criterion.</p><p>Fix \\(y\\in\\rho^{-1}(z)\\). For every lower boundary prime through \\(z\\), its root \\(a_j\\) vanishes at \\(y\\), so some upper boundary prime over it passes through \\(y\\). Primes over distinct lower primes are distinct, since finite maps preserve their dimensions. At least two upper primes meet at \\(y\\).</p><p>If at least three meet, the dlt stratum theorem makes \\(y\\) a zero-dimensional lc center; the dlt criterion gives simple normal crossings at \\(y\\).</p><p>Suppose exactly two, \\(T_1,T_2\\), meet. They lie over distinct lower primes, so each agrees locally with its entire reduced Cartier pullback and is Cartier. The normal surface \\(T_1\\) is Cohen–Macaulay. Thus the Cartier curve\n\\[\nC=T_2|_{T_1}\n\\]\nhas no embedded points. It is generically reduced by simple normal crossings at generic curve strata, hence reduced. Its irreducible components are normal curves and therefore smooth.</p><p>Two such components cannot meet at \\(y\\): their intersection would make \\(y\\) an lc center and force simple normal crossings there, whereas a simple-normal-crossing threefold pair with only two boundary branches has no zero-dimensional lc center. Thus \\(C\\) is smooth near \\(y\\).</p><p>Let \\(R=\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde U,y}\\), with \\(a,b\\) defining \\(T_1,T_2\\). Since \\(R/(a,b)\\) is regular of dimension one,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}R\n\\le\\operatorname{edim}(R/(a,b))+2\n=3=\\dim R.\n\\]\nHence \\(R\\) is regular, and \\(a,b\\) have independent cotangent images. They belong to a regular system of parameters, proving transversality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.2\\), “Vanishing at boundary intersections.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At every point \\(z\\in V\\) lying on at least two components of \\(B\\), \\( \\psi_f(1_{Z\\setminus H})(z)=0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(Z,B\\) from Lemma \\(5.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let a regular function \\(f\\) have reduced zero divisor\n  \\[\n  V=\\sum_{j\\in I}B_j,\n  \\]\n  where \\(I\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  H=\\sum_{j\\notin I}B_j.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every point \\(z\\in V\\) lying on at least two components of \\(B\\),\n\\[\n\\psi_f(1_{Z\\setminus H})(z)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the finite cover of Lemma \\(5.1\\), of degree \\(d\\). At each \\(y\\) above \\(z\\), choose analytic simple-normal-crossing coordinates. Since the cover is unramified in codimension one, vertical multiplicities remain one. Absorb a unit into one coordinate to obtain\n\\[\nf\\circ\\rho=z_1\\cdots z_r,\n\\qquad\n\\rho^{-1}H=\\{z_{r+1}\\cdots z_{r+h}=0\\},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nr\\ge1,\\qquad r+h\\ge2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(r\\ge2\\), the local Milnor fiber of the coordinate product has the homotopy type of \\((S^1)^{r-1}\\) and Euler characteristic zero. The same holds after restricting to any intersection of horizontal coordinate hyperplanes. Inclusion–exclusion gives zero on the complement of the horizontal boundary.</p><p>For \\(r=1\\), each restricted Milnor fiber is a polydisc with Euler characteristic one. Since \\(h\\ge1\\), inclusion–exclusion gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{J\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,h\\}}(-1)^{|J|}\n=(1-1)^h=0.\n\\]\nThese calculations may use adapted polydiscs or Milnor balls.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\alpha=1_{\\widetilde U\\setminus\\rho^{-1}H}.\n\\]\nOver \\(U\\setminus V\\), its finite pushforward is \\(d1_{U\\setminus H}\\): off \\(H\\), the cover is étale of degree \\(d\\), and on \\(H\\) all terms vanish. Nearby cycles depend only on restriction off \\(V\\), and proper compatibility gives\n\\[\nd\\,\\psi_f(1_{U\\setminus H})(z)\n=\n\\sum_{y\\in\\rho^{-1}(z)}\n\\psi_{f\\circ\\rho}(\\widetilde\\alpha)(y)=0.\n\\]\nThis identity concerns constructible Euler functions and requires no triviality of the covering local system. Since \\(d&gt;0\\), the conclusion follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.4\\), “The interior correction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\chi(F_x)\\ge1. \\) At a smooth point of a reduced Cartier central surface in a normal threefold, the local nearby Euler characteristic is \\(1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  q:(V,x)\\to(\\mathbb C,0)\n  \\]\n  be a flat holomorphic germ.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume its scheme central fiber \\((S,x)\\) is a normal surface with an isolated singularity and its sufficiently small nonzero fibers are smooth.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_x\\) be a sufficiently small Milnor fiber of \\(q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\chi(F_x)\\ge1.\n\\]\nAt a smooth point of a reduced Cartier central surface in a normal threefold, the local nearby Euler characteristic is \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Greuel–Steenbrink’s theorem [10], in the formulation [9, Theorem 2.18], gives\n\\[\nb_1(F_x)=0\n\\]\nwith rational coefficients for a smoothing of a normal isolated singularity; no Gorenstein hypothesis is required.</p><p>Choose a Milnor representative with smooth transverse boundary. Its interior is a smooth Stein surface, being a closed analytic submanifold of a small open ball slice. A boundary collar makes this interior homotopy equivalent to the compact Milnor representative. By the Andreotti–Frankel dimension bound [2], a complex Stein surface has the homotopy type of a CW complex of real dimension at most two. Thus\n\\[\nb_j(F_x)=0\\quad(j&gt;2).\n\\]\nThe Milnor fiber is nonempty, so\n\\[\n\\chi(F_x)=b_0(F_x)+b_2(F_x)\\ge1.\n\\]</p><p>At a smooth point of a reduced Cartier central surface, let \\(t\\) be the pulled-back base coordinate. The threefold local ring modulo \\((t)\\) is regular of dimension two. Lift generators of its maximal ideal and adjoin \\(t\\); the ambient embedding dimension is at most three, so the ambient ring is regular. The same dimension count makes \\(t\\) a regular coordinate. Hence \\(q\\) is smooth there and the local fiber is contractible.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemmas \\(6.5\\), \\(7.1\\), \\(A.1\\), Corollary \\(A.2\\), and Proposition \\(7.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every extra projective component satisfies \\( 0\\le(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2 \\le3e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S_a,B_{S_a}) \\le3\\chi_c(S_a\\setminus B_{S_a}). \\) If \\( (K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2=0 \\quad\\text{for every }a, \\) then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\to C\\), with section \\(\\Gamma\\), be a fixed-degree projective hypersurface family as supplied by Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h:Y\\to X\\) be projective birational from a normal integral threefold, isomorphic off \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  Y_0=S_0+\\sum_aS_a\n  \\]\n  is reduced Cartier, \\(S_0\\to X_0\\) is birational, and every \\(S_a\\) maps to \\(\\Gamma(0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">After shrinking, suppose \\(Y_c\\) for \\(c\\ne0\\) is normal and maps birationally to \\(X_c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be the reduced horizontal exceptional boundary, and suppose\n  \\[\n  L=K_Y+H\n  \\]\n  is rational Cartier and \\(h\\)-nef.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_S\\) be the reduced boundary on a special-fiber component \\(S\\), and assume\n  \\[\n  (S,B_S)\\text{ is log canonical},\\qquad\n  L|_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_S+B_S.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(B_{S_0}\\) is the full reduced inverse image of \\(\\Gamma(0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(c\\ne0\\), assume the full reduced inverse image \\(B_c\\) of \\(\\Gamma(c)\\) is a divisor,\n  \\[\n  (Y_c,B_c)\\text{ is log canonical},\\qquad\n  L|_{Y_c}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}K_{Y_c}+B_c.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every extra projective component satisfies\n\\[\n0\\le(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2\n\\le3e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S_a,B_{S_a})\n\\le3\\chi_c(S_a\\setminus B_{S_a}).\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2=0\n\\quad\\text{for every }a,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\beta(X_c,\\Gamma(c))=\\beta(X_0,\\Gamma(0))\n\\]\nfor all remaining \\(c\\in C\\).</p><p>The same local exceptional-square comparison is available without a projective compactification of \\(X\\): if \\(X,Y\\) are quasi-projective, \\(X\\) is normal Gorenstein, all exceptional primes map into \\(\\Gamma\\simeq C\\), and the same full-boundary, adjunction, and nefness conditions hold, then for\n\\[\nA=K_Y+H-h^*K_X\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n(A|_{Y_c})^2=(A|_{S_0})^2+\\sum_a(A|_{S_a})^2,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n(A|_{Y_c})^2=-\\beta(X_c,\\Gamma(c)),\\qquad\n(A|_{S_0})^2=-\\beta(X_0,\\Gamma(0)),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(A|_{S_a})^2=(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2.\n\\]\nSquares on nonproper surfaces are interpreted using proper curve support.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every \\(S_a\\), the adjoint divisor is nef because every curve on \\(S_a\\) is contracted by \\(h\\). Lemma \\(6.1\\) gives the surface inequality.</p><p>The integral threefold \\(Y\\) dominates the smooth curve, hence is flat by torsion-freeness over discrete valuation rings. Its fibers have pure dimension two, and it is projective over \\(C\\). Lemma \\(6.2\\) and vanishing of the extra squares give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(K_{Y_c}+B_c)^2\n&amp;=(L|_{Y_c})^2\\\\\n&amp;=(L|_{S_0})^2+\\sum_a(L|_{S_a})^2\\\\\n&amp;=(K_{S_0}+B_{S_0})^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Restrictions of \\(L\\) are nef over the corresponding \\(X_c\\). The full-boundary assumptions allow the projective formula of Proposition \\(2\\) on both the central and noncentral birational models. Since\n\\[\nK_{X_c}^2=K_{X_0}^2,\n\\]\nthe displayed equality gives equality of the local invariants.</p><p>A finite base change only repeats individual fibers and does not alter their analytic germs, so the equality concerns the original fiber singularities as well.</p><p>The nonproper local variant is Lemma \\(6.4\\), based on the supported-square specialization of Lemma \\(6.3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.5\\), “The surface inequality.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( 0\\le(K_S+B)^2 \\le3e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S,B) \\le3\\chi_c(S^\\circ). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a normal projective complex surface.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a reduced effective Weil divisor, possibly empty.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((S,B)\\) is log canonical and \\(K_S+B\\) is rational Cartier and nef.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(S^\\circ=S\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(e_{\\mathrm{orb}}\\) denote Langer’s orbifold Euler number.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n0\\le(K_S+B)^2\n\\le3e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S,B)\n\\le3\\chi_c(S^\\circ).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Nefness on the projective surface implies pseudoeffectivity and nonnegative self-intersection. The latter may also be checked on a projective resolution, where the pullback remains nef and has the same square.</p><p>The pseudoeffective form of Langer’s inequality gives\n\\[\n(K_S+B)^2\\le3e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S,B)\n\\]\nby [18, Corollary 5.2].</p><p>Smooth boundary points have orbifold weight zero. At every singular point of the pair on the boundary, the local orbifold number also vanishes, because a boundary branch has coefficient one and the pair is log canonical [18, Theorem 7.6].</p><p>For\n\\[\nZ=\\operatorname{Sing}S\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}B,\n\\]\nthe weighted formula is therefore\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{orb}}(S,B)\n=\n\\chi_c(S^\\circ)\n+\n\\sum_{x\\in Z}\\bigl(e_{\\mathrm{orb}}(x;S,0)-1\\bigr).\n\\]\nEvery summand is nonpositive by [18, Definition 3.4 and Corollary 7.8]. This proves the final inequality, including all singular-point corrections.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.1\\), “Square specialization.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(c\\in C\\), \\( (L|_{Y_c})^2=\\sum_jm_j(L|_{S_j})^2. \\) If \\(Y_0\\) is reduced, all \\(m_j=1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q:Y\\to C\\) be flat projective over a smooth connected complex curve, with pure two-dimensional fibers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a rational Cartier divisor on \\(Y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  [Y_0]=\\sum_jm_j[S_j]\n  \\]\n  for the cycle of a fiber.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(c\\in C\\),\n\\[\n(L|_{Y_c})^2=\\sum_jm_j(L|_{S_j})^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(Y_0\\) is reduced, all \\(m_j=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(r&gt;0\\) with \\(rL\\) Cartier. Flatness and properness make\n\\[\n\\chi\\bigl(Y_c,\\mathcal O_Y(nrL)|_{Y_c}\\bigr)\n\\]\nindependent of \\(c\\) for every integer \\(n\\) [29, Lemma 36.32.2].</p><p>This is a polynomial in \\(n\\) of degree at most two, with quadratic coefficient\n\\[\n\\frac{r^2(L|_{Y_c})^2}{2}.\n\\]\nIts leading term is the sum of the leading terms on the two-dimensional components, weighted by their generic lengths [29, Lemmas 33.45.1, 33.45.2, and 33.45.6]. Compare quadratic coefficients and divide by \\(r^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.1\\), “Specialization of a supported square.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The number \\( Q_c(A)=\\sum_jm_j(A|_{S_j})^2 \\) is independent of \\(c\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q:Y\\to C\\) be dominant from an integral normal \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial quasi-projective complex threefold to a smooth connected algebraic curve.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  A=\\sum_i a_iE_i\n  \\]\n  be a rational divisor whose support is proper over \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write the fundamental cycle of a fiber as\n  \\[\n  [Y_c]=\\sum_jm_j[S_j].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(S_j\\) is proper, use its usual projective intersection square.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(S_j\\) is not proper, then \\(S_j\\) is not a component of \\(A\\), and \\(A|_{S_j}\\) has proper curve support; its square is the degree of that curve cycle intersected with the rational line bundle \\(A|_{S_j}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The number\n\\[\nQ_c(A)=\\sum_jm_j(A|_{S_j})^2\n\\]\nis independent of \\(c\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The morphism is flat because its local rings are torsion-free over the discrete valuation rings of \\(C\\). Its fibers have pure dimension two. Every vertical \\(E_i\\) is proper. Every horizontal \\(E_i\\) is projective and flat over \\(C\\): properness plus quasi-projectivity gives projectivity, and torsion-freeness gives flatness. All intersections below have proper support.</p><p>Multiply by common positive integers to make the divisors Cartier, and divide after intersection. Commutativity and additivity give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_c(A)\n&amp;=\\sum_i a_i\\sum_jm_j\\deg\\bigl(A\\cdot E_i\\cdot[S_j]\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;=\\sum_i a_i\\deg\\bigl(A|_{E_i}\\cdot Y_c|_{E_i}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor distinct \\(E_i,S_j\\), the two orders of restriction describe the same proper intersection curves with multiplicities. If \\(E_i=S_j\\), everything occurs on a proper surface and uses its normal-bundle class. This also justifies the formula for nonproper fiber components.</p><p>If \\(E_i\\) is vertical, the line bundle of \\(Y_c\\) restricts trivially to it, since it is pulled back from a point divisor on \\(C\\) along a constant map. Its term is zero, including when \\(E_i\\subset Y_c\\).</p><p>If \\(E_i\\) is horizontal, the term is the degree of the fixed rational line bundle \\(A|_{E_i}\\) on the scheme fiber \\((E_i)_c\\). This is constant in a projective flat curve family. For example, it is the coefficient of \\(n\\) in the Euler characteristic of a Cartier multiple of \\(nA|_{E_i}\\), whose fiberwise Euler characteristic is constant.</p><p>For the second proof given in the paper, take an integral normal projective compactification\n\\[\n\\overline Y\\to C.\n\\]\nThe support of \\(A\\) is already closed in \\(\\overline Y\\), because it is proper over the separated base. A Cartier multiple of \\(A\\) extends by gluing to the zero divisor on\n\\[\n\\overline Y\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}(A).\n\\]\nThe compactification is flat. Lemma \\(6.2\\) makes the extended fiber square constant. On components contained in \\(\\overline Y\\setminus Y\\), its restriction is trivial and its square is zero. On components meeting \\(Y\\), its square is exactly the supported intersection number above. Hence the projective identity is the desired local identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Corollary \\(A.2\\), “The local exceptional-square comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( (A|_{Y_c})^2=(A|_{S_0})^2+\\sum_a(A|_{S_a})^2. \\) The first two squares are respectively \\( -\\beta(X_c,\\Gamma(c)),\\qquad -\\beta(X_0,\\Gamma(0)), \\) and \\( (A|_{S_a})^2=(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2. \\) Thus vanishing of all …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h:Y\\to X\\) satisfy the log-model assumptions of Proposition \\(4\\), except that \\(X\\to C\\) need not be proper.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X,Y\\) are quasi-projective and all exceptional prime divisors map into \\(\\Gamma\\simeq C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  A=K_Y+H-h^*K_X.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(Y_0=S_0+\\sum_aS_a\\) is reduced and nearby fibers are irreducible.</p><p class=\"li\">For the identification with \\(\\beta\\), impose the full-boundary, log-canonicity, adjunction, and nefness assumptions of Proposition \\(6\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(A|_{Y_c})^2=(A|_{S_0})^2+\\sum_a(A|_{S_a})^2.\n\\]\nThe first two squares are respectively\n\\[\n-\\beta(X_c,\\Gamma(c)),\\qquad -\\beta(X_0,\\Gamma(0)),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(A|_{S_a})^2=(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2.\n\\]\nThus vanishing of all extra squares proves constancy of the local invariant without projective compactification of \\(X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The divisor \\(A\\) is exceptional and has proper support over the section, because \\(h\\) is projective. Apply Lemma \\(6.3\\).</p><p>On each \\(S_a\\), a divisor pulled back from \\(X\\) has trivial line-bundle class. Adjunction therefore gives\n\\[\n(A|_{S_a})^2=(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(S_0\\) and a nearby fiber, pull back to a good resolution of the corresponding germ. As in the projective computation in Proposition \\(2\\), log canonicity makes the difference from the pulled-back logarithmic canonical divisor an effective exceptional negative part, orthogonal to the relatively nef part. The pullback of \\(A\\) is the numerical exceptional representative of that nef part. Its properly supported square is \\(-\\beta\\).</p><p>No global canonical square of \\(X_c\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem \\(7.3\\), “Constancy of the logarithmic invariant.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently small \\(t\\), \\( \\beta(X_t,0)=\\beta(X_0,0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F(x,t)=f_t(x)\\) be a holomorphic family of isolated hypersurface singularities in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(f_t(0)=0\\) and that its positive Milnor number is constant.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(X_t=\\{f_t=0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((X_0,0)\\) is non-log-canonical.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta=-P_{\\mathrm{loc}}^2\\) be the invariant of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently small \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\beta(X_t,0)=\\beta(X_0,0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By the scalar curve reduction of Proposition \\(2\\), it suffices to establish \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\).</p><p>Consider a polynomial family over a smooth algebraic curve with marked non-log-canonical fiber. Log canonicity is constant by Proposition \\(2\\), so all relevant germs are non-log-canonical. Its compactification from Proposition \\(2\\) has precisely the prescribed singularities, smooth complement of the section, constant punctured-fiber Euler characteristic, and constant canonical square.</p><p>Construct the model of Proposition \\(3\\), with its finite smooth-curve base change. Write\n\\[\nY_0=S_0+\\sum_aS_a,\\qquad\nL=K_Y+H,\\qquad\nS^\\circ=S\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}B_S.\n\\]\nProposition \\(4\\) verifies adjunction and the full-boundary conditions:\n\\[\nS_0^\\circ\\simeq X_0\\setminus\\Gamma,\n\\qquad\nY_c\\setminus H\\simeq X_c\\setminus\\Gamma\n\\quad(c\\ne0).\n\\]\nThe first open surface is smooth. Every isolated interior singularity of another component is smoothed by the family, and Proposition \\(5\\) gives a nonnegative correction.</p><p>Apply its Euler comparison. Constancy of the punctured-fiber Euler characteristic cancels the \\(S_0^\\circ\\) contribution:\n\\[\n0=\\sum_a\\chi_c(S_a^\\circ)+\\delta,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\delta=\n\\sum_a\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{Sing}(S_a)\\cap S_a^\\circ}\n\\bigl(\\chi(F_x)-1\\bigr)\\ge0.\n\\]\nEach \\(S_a\\) is projective with nef adjoint divisor. Proposition \\(6\\) gives\n\\[\n0\\le(K_{S_a}+B_{S_a})^2\\le3\\chi_c(S_a^\\circ).\n\\]\nThus every term in the Euler equality is nonnegative. Every extra logarithmic square vanishes.</p><p>Proposition \\(6\\) now proves equality of the marked and nearby general Wahl invariants. The finite base change repeats the individual germs, so this is the desired algebraic assertion for the original family. Proposition \\(2\\) transfers the conclusion to the holomorphic family.</p><p>Alternatively, after establishing \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\), Lemma \\(7.1\\) supplies the final curve comparison without constructibility of the scalar invariant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.4\\), “Comparison within an irreducible jet component.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The two points have the same Wahl invariant.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_\\mu\\) be the locally closed jet locus of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the algebraic assertion \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\) stated there.</p><p class=\"li\">Let two points of one irreducible component of \\(U_\\mu\\) be given, and assume one defines a non-log-canonical surface germ.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two points have the same Wahl invariant. Consequently, the scalar curve reduction of Proposition \\(2\\) also holds without invoking constructibility of the Wahl invariant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(Z\\) be the irreducible component and \\(a_0,a_1\\in Z\\) the points. If they coincide, there is nothing to prove.</p><p>Resolve a projective closure of \\(Z\\), obtaining a smooth irreducible projective \\(\\widetilde Z\\) with proper surjective map to the closure. Choose a point over each \\(a_i\\). If \\(\\dim Z=1\\), use \\(\\widetilde Z\\). If \\(\\dim Z\\ge2\\), general sufficiently ample hypersurfaces through the two chosen points cut out a smooth irreducible projective curve through them. The systems can separate first jets at those points, so general members are smooth there; Bertini applies away from the finite base locus. Iterate smoothness and irreducibility.</p><p>The complement of the inverse image of \\(Z\\) is closed and misses the chosen points. Its intersection with the curve is finite. Delete those points and, if necessary, take an affine open containing both chosen points. This gives a smooth connected algebraic curve \\(C'\\to U_\\mu\\) whose image contains \\(a_0,a_1\\).</p><p>Pulling back the universal polynomial gives regular coefficients and constant Milnor number. By persistence of log canonicity in Proposition \\(2\\), every germ on \\(C'\\) is non-log-canonical. Apply \\((\\mathrm{AC})\\) with any point as marked point. Its invariant equals nearby general values. The exceptional algebraic subset of a curve is finite; shrinking an analytic disk avoids its other points. Hence the invariant is locally constant throughout \\(C'\\), and its values at the two chosen points agree.</p><p>Finally, the finitely many irreducible components of \\(U_\\mu\\) not containing \\(a_0\\) form a closed subset missing \\(a_0\\). A sufficiently small analytic neighborhood avoids them. Every nearby jet \\(a(t)\\) therefore shares an irreducible component with \\(a_0\\), and its Wahl invariant agrees with the central value. Individual finite determinacy in Proposition \\(2\\) transfers the equality back to the original holomorphic family.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking and making a finite base change \\(t=u^e\\), there is a proper modification \\( b:N\\to X'=\\{F(x,u^e)=0\\} \\) such that \\(N\\to\\Delta_u\\) is smooth, \\(b\\) is an isomorphism off the origin section, and its …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F(x,t)=f_t(x)\\) satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(X_t=\\{f_t=0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(X_0\\) is non-log-canonical and \\(\\beta(X_t,0)\\) is constant.</p><p class=\"li\">A surface resolution is semigood when its reduced exceptional divisor has normal crossings, allowing self-nodes of irreducible components.</p><p class=\"li\">In a smooth surface family, a divisor has relative normal crossings if locally there are coordinates \\((z_1,z_2,u)\\) in which the family map is \\(u\\), and the divisor is \\(z_1=0\\) or \\(z_1z_2=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking and making a finite base change \\(t=u^e\\), there is a proper modification\n\\[\nb:N\\to X'=\\{F(x,u^e)=0\\}\n\\]\nsuch that \\(N\\to\\Delta_u\\) is smooth, \\(b\\) is an isomorphism off the origin section, and its reduced exceptional divisor \\(E\\) has relative normal crossings. For every \\(u\\), \\(b_u\\) is the minimal semigood resolution and \\(E|_{N_u}\\) is its full reduced exceptional divisor.</p>", "proof": "<p>The zero family is a flat deformation of a normal Gorenstein surface singularity by Proposition \\(1\\), with exactly one singularity in each chosen representative. Constancy of the invariant at the section is therefore constancy of the sum over fiber singularities used in Okuma’s notation.</p><p>First verify the small-deformation setup of [23, Sections 3–4]. Resolve the analytic total space and principalize the pulled-back ideal of the section on the smooth resolution, using [34, Theorems 2.0.1 and 2.0.3, Lemma 4.0.3]. The composite is proper and isomorphic off the section, where the total space is smooth and the ideal is the unit ideal. The full reduced inverse image of the section is simple normal crossing and proper over the disk.</p><p>Critical loci for the parameter map on the resolution and on closed intersections of exceptional components lie in this proper support. Those intersections are smooth. On each, a critical component has constant parameter value; an intersection contained in a fiber also has one value. Properness makes their images closed analytic subsets of the disk. After shrinking, only \\(0\\) can occur. Over the punctured disk, the resolution is therefore smooth with relative normal-crossing exceptional divisor, giving a locally trivial deformation of that divisor and the punctured-disk weak simultaneous resolution required by Okuma.</p><p>Okuma’s Theorem 1.2 [23] gives, after finite base change, a simultaneous resolution with minimal semigood fiber resolutions and a reduced divisor \\(E\\) whose restrictions are the full reduced exceptional divisors over the distinguished non-log-canonical section. This section is the origin section.</p><p>Normalize a branch of the finite base change. A local parameter makes the map \\(t=u^e\\). Flatness and smooth fibers make the resulting resolved family smooth over the disk. Cartier-divisor restrictions commute with the further base change; in particular, the restriction at \\(u=0\\) remains reduced. The divisor \\(E\\) contains no fiber and is relative effective Cartier, with support\n\\[\nb^{-1}(\\{0\\}\\times\\Delta_u).\n\\]</p><p>It remains to prove relative normal crossings. Take the fixed closed ball \\(B\\) of Proposition \\(1\\), and put\n\\[\nN_B=b^{-1}\\bigl(X'\\cap(B\\times\\Delta_u)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts projection is a proper smooth submersion with boundary: near the sphere, \\(b\\) is an isomorphism and Proposition \\(1\\) gives uniform transversality. Hence \\(\\chi((N_B)_u)\\) is constant.</p><p>The complement of \\(E_u\\) is isomorphic to\n\\[\n(X_{u^e}\\cap B)\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nIts compactly supported Euler characteristic is zero by Proposition \\(1\\). Additivity gives\n\\[\n\\chi(E_u)=\\chi((N_B)_u),\n\\]\nso the compact reduced nodal curves \\(E_u\\) have constant Euler characteristic.</p><p>At a smooth point of \\(E_0\\), the divisor is already smooth over the parameter. At a node, the holomorphic Morse lemma with parameter gives\n\\[\nz_1z_2=a(u),\\qquad a(0)=0.\n\\]\nThere are finitely many nodes. Choose disjoint small node neighborhoods with transverse boundaries. Smoothness near the remaining points of \\(E_0\\), and properness of \\(E\\to\\Delta_u\\), exclude new nodes elsewhere after shrinking. The remainder is a proper smooth family with boundary and unchanged topology.</p><p>A crossing in a node neighborhood has Euler characteristic one; its smoothing is an annulus and has Euler characteristic zero. For general small \\(u\\),\n\\[\n\\chi(E_u)\n=\n\\chi(E_0)\n-\n\\#\\{\\text{nodes with }a\\not\\equiv0\\}.\n\\]\nThis includes self-nodes and needs no global component labeling. Constancy forces every smoothing function to vanish identically. The resulting charts prove relative normal crossings near \\(E_0\\); properness extends the conclusion over a smaller disk.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.1\\), Corollary \\(9.2\\), Proposition \\(9.3\\), and Lemma \\(9.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Either condition supplies a jointly continuous ambient right-trivialization fixing the section and equal to the identity at the central parameter.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G(x,u)\\) be holomorphic near the origin section in \\(\\mathbb C^n\\times D\\), with \\(G(0,u)=0\\) and no spatial critical point off that section.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider either of the following sufficient geometric conditions:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. The pair\n     \\[\n     \\bigl(\\{G=0\\}\\setminus\\{x=0\\},\\{x=0\\}\\bigr)\n     \\]\n     satisfies Whitney conditions \\((a)\\) and \\((b)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. The zero family admits a proper holomorphic map \\(b:M\\to\\{G=0\\}\\) over \\(D\\), isomorphic off the section, such that \\(M\\to D\\) is smooth and \\(b^{-1}(\\{x=0\\})\\) is a relative normal-crossing divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">In condition \\(2\\), assume the fixed-ball transversality hypotheses stated in Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Either condition supplies a jointly continuous ambient right-trivialization fixing the section and equal to the identity at the central parameter.</p><p>On fixed closed-ball tubes, this trivialization can be chosen as homeomorphisms\n\\[\nH_u:T_0\\to T_u,\\qquad\nG(H_u(x),u)=G(x,0),\n\\]\nwith jointly continuous inverses.</p><p>If \\(G(x,u)=F(x,u^e)\\) and these tube trivializations exist after the finite base change \\(t=u^e\\), then \\(F\\) is ambiently topologically right-trivial over the original disk, without requiring deck-equivariance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(9.1\\) converts complete transport on punctured zero fibers into an ambient function-preserving tube trivialization. Lemma \\(9.2\\) supplies the required transport under Whitney regularity; Lemma \\(9.3\\) supplies it from a proper smooth resolved family with relative normal crossings. Lemma \\(9.4\\) removes a finite disk base change on the exact tube domains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.1\\), “Ambient lifting on a closed-ball tube.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After shrinking \\(D\\), there are homeomorphisms \\( H_u:T_0\\to T_u \\) such that \\( H_0=\\operatorname{id},\\qquad H_u(0)=0,\\qquad G(H_u(x),u)=G(x,0). \\) The maps and inverses are jointly continuous.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be holomorphic near \\(B\\times D\\), where \\(B\\subset\\mathbb C^n\\) is a closed ball centered at \\(0\\), and \\(D\\subset\\mathbb C\\) is a disk centered at \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(G(0,u)=0\\) and \\(d_xG(x,u)\\ne0\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For some \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), assume levels \\(G(\\cdot,u)=c\\), \\(|c|&lt;\\eta\\), meet \\(\\partial B\\) transversely.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  T_u=\\{x\\in B:|G(x,u)|&lt;\\eta\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Z^*=\\{(x,u)\\in(B\\setminus\\{0\\})\\times D:G(x,u)=0\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose smooth real fields \\(v_1,v_i\\) on \\(Z^*\\), tangent to its sphere boundary, project to \\(1,i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume transport of\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Re}(u)v_1+\\operatorname{Im}(u)v_i\n  \\]\n  for time one from parameter \\(0\\), and reverse transport from \\(u\\), exist for the entire segment \\([0,u]\\) in \\(Z^*\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking \\(D\\), there are homeomorphisms\n\\[\nH_u:T_0\\to T_u\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nH_0=\\operatorname{id},\\qquad H_u(0)=0,\\qquad\nG(H_u(x),u)=G(x,0).\n\\]\nThe maps and inverses are jointly continuous. Restriction to tube interiors gives a parameter-preserving ambient homeomorphism near the origin section.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the ambient tube minus the section, \\((G,u)\\) is a smooth submersion, including on the sphere boundary. Extend each \\(v_a\\), \\(a\\in\\{1,i\\}\\), to a smooth ambient field \\(V_a\\) satisfying\n\\[\ndu(V_a)=a,\\qquad dG(V_a)=0,\n\\]\nand tangent to the sphere boundary.</p><p>The prescribed extension along \\(Z^*\\) can be made in submersion coordinates, using boundary coordinates where necessary. Equivalently, subtract a preliminary lift and extend the remaining section of \\(\\ker d(G,u)\\) from the closed submanifold \\(Z^*\\) of the axis complement. A partition of unity patches these extensions because projection, tangency, and prescribed-value conditions are affine linear. No extension to the axis is asserted.</p><p>For \\(u\\in D\\), integrate\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(u)V_1+\\operatorname{Im}(u)V_i\n\\]\nfor time one from \\((x,0)\\). For \\(x\\ne0\\) on the zero level, existence is the hypothesis. If \\(G(x,0)=c\\ne0\\), the trajectory remains in\n\\[\n\\{(y,s):y\\in B,\\ s\\in[0,u],\\ G(y,s)=c\\},\n\\]\na compact set missing the axis. Smooth continuation and boundary tangency give existence for the full interval. Reverse transport gives an inverse on \\(T_u\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Both maps depend smoothly on arguments off the axis and preserve \\(G\\).</p><p>Set \\(H_u(0)=0\\). To prove joint continuity there, take \\(x_j\\to0\\) and \\(u_j\\to u\\). Any subsequential limit \\(y\\) of \\(H_{u_j}(x_j)\\) lies in \\(B\\), and function preservation gives \\(G(y,u)=0\\). If \\(y\\ne0\\), smooth dependence of reverse transport near \\((y,u)\\) would make \\(x_j\\) converge to its nonzero endpoint over \\(0\\), a contradiction. Thus every limit is \\(0\\).</p><p>For inverse continuity, if \\(y_j\\in T_{u_j}\\) tends to \\(0\\), any nonzero subsequential limit of \\(H_{u_j}^{-1}(y_j)\\) contradicts smooth forward transport. Hence both families are jointly continuous everywhere. Reverse transport proves surjectivity onto exactly \\(T_u\\).</p><p>Tube interiors are open neighborhoods of the respective origins, and their union is an open neighborhood of the section.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Corollary \\(9.2\\), “Whitney regular zero fibers.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The family \\(G\\) is ambiently topologically right-trivial near the section, with central map the identity.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G(x,u)\\), \\(x\\in\\mathbb C^n\\), be holomorphic, with \\(G(0,u)=0\\) and no spatial critical point off the origin section near \\((0,0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\bigl(\\{G=0\\}\\setminus\\{x=0\\},\\{x=0\\}\\bigr)\n  \\]\n  satisfies Whitney conditions \\((a)\\) and \\((b)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(G\\) is ambiently topologically right-trivial near the section, with central map the identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\rho(x,u)=\\|x\\|^2\\). We claim \\((u,\\rho)\\) is a real submersion on the punctured zero locus sufficiently near \\((0,0)\\).</p><p>Otherwise choose failure points \\((x_j,u_j)\\to(0,0)\\). Pass to a subsequence such that\n\\[\nx_j/\\|x_j\\|\\to v,\n\\]\nthe real tangent spaces of the total zero locus tend to \\(L\\), and the tangent spaces of its fibers tend to \\(Q\\).</p><p>Whitney \\((a)\\) puts both real base directions in \\(L\\). The fiber tangent spaces have dimension \\(2n-2\\) and lie in \\(\\ker du\\), so\n\\[\nQ=L\\cap\\ker du\n\\]\nby inclusion and dimension. Whitney \\((b)\\), applied to secants from \\((0,u_j)\\), gives\n\\[\n(v,0)\\in L,\n\\]\nhence \\((v,0)\\in Q\\). Failure of submersivity says \\(d\\rho\\) vanishes on every fiber tangent space, making \\((v,0)\\) orthogonal to \\(Q\\), contrary to \\(\\|v\\|=1\\).</p><p>Choose a small closed ball and disk in this neighborhood. Patch smooth local right inverses of \\(d(u,\\rho)\\) to obtain lifts \\(v_1,v_i\\) with\n\\[\nd\\rho(v_a)=0.\n\\]\nTheir trajectories preserve positive radius, hence remain, along a fixed parameter segment, in a compact set disjoint from the axis. Transport is complete in both directions, and the fields are tangent to the sphere.</p><p>Zero levels are transverse to the sphere; compactness gives the same for small nonzero levels and parameters. Lemma \\(9.1\\) applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.3\\), “Lifts supplied by a resolution.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The family \\(G\\) has the tube trivialization of Lemma \\(9.1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) satisfy the analytic and transversality hypotheses of Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose there is a proper holomorphic map\n  \\[\n  b:M\\to\\{G=0\\}\n  \\]\n  over \\(D\\), such that \\(q:M\\to D\\) is smooth, \\(b\\) is an isomorphism off the origin section, and\n  \\[\n  E=b^{-1}(\\{x=0\\})\n  \\]\n  is a relative normal-crossing divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Relative normal crossings means that in coordinates consisting of fiber coordinates and \\(u\\), the support is a union of fiber-coordinate hyperplanes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(G\\) has the tube trivialization of Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the ball and a smaller disk within representatives of \\(b\\). Above the closed ball, \\(q\\) is a smooth submersion with boundary: near the sphere \\(b\\) is an isomorphism and the fibers are transverse.</p><p>Relative normal-crossing coordinates give local lifts of the two real parameter directions tangent to every branch of \\(E\\). Use submersion coordinates away from \\(E\\), and boundary-submersion coordinates near the sphere. A partition of unity gives smooth lifts tangent to both \\(E\\) and the sphere boundary.</p><p>Properness makes the inverse image of the closed ball over each compact parameter segment compact. The lifts are complete along that segment in both directions. Their flows preserve \\(E\\) and its complement: tangency preserves each local branch, and uniqueness in both directions prevents a complementary trajectory from reaching \\(E\\).</p><p>Push the fields down through the isomorphism off \\(E\\). They are the complete fields required by Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.4\\), “Removal of a finite disk base change.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The original family \\(F\\) is ambiently topologically right-trivial over its original disk, by a section-preserving homeomorphism whose central map is the identity.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F(x,t)\\) be holomorphic with \\(F(0,t)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that after \\(t=u^e\\), \\(e\\ge1\\), the family\n  \\[\n  G(x,u)=F(x,u^e)\n  \\]\n  has the tube trivialization \\(H_u\\) of Lemma \\(9.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The original family \\(F\\) is ambiently topologically right-trivial over its original disk, by a section-preserving homeomorphism whose central map is the identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\exp(2\\pi i/e).\n\\]\nFor real \\(s\\ge0\\) sufficiently small, define\n\\[\nJ_s=H_{\\zeta s}^{-1}\\circ H_s:T_0\\to T_0.\n\\]\nThis is defined on exact common domains, since \\(T_s=T_{\\zeta s}\\). It preserves \\(f_0=F(\\cdot,0)\\), \\(J_0=\\operatorname{id}\\), and \\(J_s,J_s^{-1}\\) are jointly continuous.</p><p>For\n\\[\nt=r^e\\exp(i\\theta),\\qquad r&gt;0,\\quad0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi,\n\\]\ndefine\n\\[\n\\phi_t=H_{r\\exp(i\\theta/e)}\\circ J_{r\\theta/(2\\pi)}.\n\\]\nAt the angular seam,\n\\[\nH_{\\zeta r}\\circ J_r=H_r,\n\\]\nso the two endpoint formulas agree. The inverse is\n\\[\n\\phi_t^{-1}\n=\nJ_{r\\theta/(2\\pi)}^{-1}\n\\circ H_{r\\exp(i\\theta/e)}^{-1},\n\\]\nand its seam identity is\n\\[\nJ_r^{-1}\\circ H_{\\zeta r}^{-1}=H_r^{-1}.\n\\]\nBoth maps therefore glue.</p><p>At \\(r=0\\), all constituent maps are the identity, independently of \\(\\theta\\). Joint continuity of the families and their inverses gives continuity on the polar disk with its central circle collapsed. Set \\(\\phi_0=\\operatorname{id}\\).</p><p>The source is \\(T_0\\), the target is\n\\[\n\\{x\\in B:|f_t(x)|&lt;\\eta\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nf_t\\circ\\phi_t=f_0\\circ J_{r\\theta/(2\\pi)}=f_0,\n\\qquad \\phi_t(0)=0.\n\\]\nRestrict to interiors.</p><p>A finite map between smooth pointed disks has the form \\(t=u^e\\) after a coordinate change: absorb a holomorphic \\(e\\)-th root of its nonvanishing unit factor into \\(u\\). Thus the argument applies to a normalized branch of a resolution base change and requires no deck-equivariance.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition \\(10.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The family \\(F\\) is topologically right-trivial over a sufficiently small parameter disk, with central map the identity and jointly continuous maps and inverses.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F(x,t)=f_t(x)\\) be holomorphic in three spatial variables.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(f_t(0)=0\\), that \\(f_t\\) has an isolated critical point at \\(0\\), and that \\(\\mu(f_t,0)\\) is constant.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume either\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{mult}_0(f_0)=2\n  \\]\n  or the normal surface \\(X_0=\\{f_0=0\\}\\) is log canonical.</p><p class=\"li\">For a germ in \\(n\\) variables, let \\(\\mu^{(j)}\\) be the Milnor number of restriction to a general \\(j\\)-dimensional linear subspace, and put\n  \\[\n  \\mu^*=(\\mu^{(1)},\\ldots,\\mu^{(n)}).\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\mu^{(n)}=\\mu\\) and \\(\\mu^{(1)}=\\operatorname{mult}_0(f)-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(F\\) is topologically right-trivial over a sufficiently small parameter disk, with central map the identity and jointly continuous maps and inverses.</p>", "proof": "<p>We use equimultiplicity of \\(\\mu\\)-constant families [6, Theorem 1.1], and Teissier’s criterion that constancy of \\(\\mu^*\\) implies Whitney regularity along the section [28, Chapter II, Theorem 3.9]. The smooth-complement hypothesis is supplied by Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p>Suppose first that \\(\\operatorname{mult}_0(f_0)=2\\). Choose a spatial direction with nonzero second derivative. The implicit function theorem solves the corresponding first-derivative equation as\n\\[\nz=a(y,t),\\qquad y\\in\\mathbb C^2,\\qquad a(0,t)=0.\n\\]\nTranslate \\(z\\) by \\(a(y,t)\\) and take a holomorphic square root of the resulting nonvanishing quadratic coefficient. This gives origin-preserving biholomorphic coordinates, holomorphic in \\(t\\), in which\n\\[\nf_t=z^2+g_t(y).\n\\]\nThe Jacobian quotient is that of \\(g_t\\), so \\(g_t\\) is isolated and\n\\[\n\\mu(g_t)=\\mu(f_t).\n\\]\nEquimultiplicity makes\n\\[\n\\mu^*(g_t)=\\bigl(\\operatorname{mult}_0(g_t)-1,\\mu(g_t)\\bigr)\n\\]\nconstant. Teissier’s criterion and Proposition \\(9\\) give a joint right-trivialization of \\(g_t\\). Extend by the identity in \\(z\\), then conjugate by the coordinate changes at \\(0\\) and \\(t\\). The resulting central map is the identity.</p><p>Now assume \\(X_0\\) is log canonical. Adjunction and inversion of adjunction identify this with log canonicity of\n\\[\n(\\mathbb C^3,X_0).\n\\]\nThe different on a normal Cartier hypersurface in a smooth ambient space is zero. These algebraic statements apply to the analytic germ by its right-equivalence to a high polynomial jet, as in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p>Put \\(m=\\operatorname{mult}_0(f_0)\\). Blowing up the origin gives exceptional-plane discrepancy \\(2-m\\), so \\(m\\le3\\). Since \\(0\\) is critical, \\(m\\ge2\\), and \\(m=2\\) is settled.</p><p>For \\(m=3\\), let \\(\\widetilde X_0\\) be the strict transform and \\(P\\simeq\\mathbb P^2\\) the exceptional plane. The crepant boundary is\n\\[\n\\widetilde X_0+P.\n\\]\nAdjunction to \\(P\\) makes \\((P,C)\\) log canonical, where\n\\[\nC=\\widetilde X_0|_P\n\\]\nis the tangent cubic with its scheme multiplicities. Log-canonical boundary coefficients are at most one, so \\(C\\) is reduced.</p><p>Equimultiplicity keeps the order equal to three. Choose a projective line transverse to \\(C\\) at three distinct smooth points. This condition persists for the cubic initial forms for small \\(t\\). The corresponding, and hence general, plane sections are ordinary triple points. Their Milnor number is four: the two quadratic initial forms of their partial derivatives have no common projective zero, so their local intersection multiplicity is \\(2\\cdot2\\).</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\n\\mu^*(f_t)=(2,4,\\mu(f_0))\n\\]\non a small disk. Teissier’s criterion gives Whitney regularity, and Proposition \\(9\\) supplies the right-trivialization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition \\(B.1\\), with Remark \\(B.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After shrinking, there is an ambient homeomorphism germ \\( H(x,u)=(H_u(x),u) \\) such that \\( G(H_u(x),u)=G(x,0),\\qquad H_u(0)=0,\\qquad H_0=\\operatorname{id}, \\) with \\(H,H^{-1}\\) jointly continuous.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be holomorphic near \\(\\{0\\}\\times D\\subset\\mathbb C^n\\times D\\), where \\(D\\) is a disk centered at \\(0\\), and assume \\(G(0,u)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  A=\\{0\\}\\times D,\\qquad X^*=\\{G=0\\}\\setminus A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write real tangent vectors in complex coordinates as \\((w,a)\\in\\mathbb C^n\\times\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(d_xG\\ne0\\) off \\(A\\) and, at every point of \\(A\\),\n  \\[\n  \\frac{|G_u(x,u)|}{\\|d_xG(x,u)\\|}\\longrightarrow0\n  \\quad\\text{as }(x,u)\\to A,\\ x\\ne0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(a=1,i\\), suppose \\(X^*\\) carries a smooth real field \\(v_a=(w_a,a)\\) satisfying:\n  \\[\n  dG(v_a)=0,\\qquad w_a(x,u)\\to0\\text{ at }A,\n  \\]\n  and no trajectory of \\(v_a\\) has a finite-time endpoint in \\(A\\) inside the representative, in either time direction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After shrinking, there is an ambient homeomorphism germ\n\\[\nH(x,u)=(H_u(x),u)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nG(H_u(x),u)=G(x,0),\\qquad\nH_u(0)=0,\\qquad H_0=\\operatorname{id},\n\\]\nwith \\(H,H^{-1}\\) jointly continuous.</p><p>If the trajectory condition holds for every constant real combination of \\(v_1,v_i\\), transport may instead be taken along radial segments \\([0,u]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On the ambient complement of \\(A\\), put\n\\[\nk_a\n=\n-aG_u\n\\frac{(\\overline{G_{x_1}},\\ldots,\\overline{G_{x_n}})}\n     {\\|d_xG\\|^2},\n\\qquad\nK_a=(k_a,a).\n\\]\nThese smooth real fields satisfy \\(dG(K_a)=0\\). The gradient assumption gives \\(k_a\\to0\\) at \\(A\\).</p><p>On \\(X^*\\),\n\\[\ns_a=w_a-k_a\n\\]\nis a section of the complex bundle\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=\\ker d_xG\n\\]\nand tends to zero at \\(A\\).</p><p>Extend the correction while preserving this vanishing. The smooth \\(X^*\\) is closed in the axis complement. Choose a smooth tubular retraction\n\\[\nr:U\\to X^*\n\\]\nwith variable radius small enough that\n\\[\n\\|z-r(z)\\|&lt;\\tfrac12\\operatorname{dist}(r(z),A).\n\\]\nTake a smooth \\(\\chi\\) on the axis complement, equal to one on \\(X^*\\), with values in \\([0,1]\\) and support in \\(U\\). With Hermitian orthogonal projection \\(\\pi_{\\mathcal K_z}\\), define\n\\[\n\\widetilde s_a(z)\n=\n\\chi(z)\\pi_{\\mathcal K_z}(s_a(r(z)))\n\\quad(z\\in U),\n\\]\nand zero outside \\(U\\).</p><p>This is smooth, takes values in \\(\\mathcal K\\), and agrees with \\(s_a\\) on \\(X^*\\). As \\(z\\) approaches a point of \\(A\\) within \\(U\\), the tubular bound makes \\(r(z)\\) approach the same point. Since projection has norm at most one,\n\\[\n\\widetilde s_a(z)\\to0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nW_a=(k_a+\\widetilde s_a,a)\n\\]\nextends continuously across \\(A\\) by \\(W_a=(0,a)\\). It is smooth off \\(A\\), agrees with \\(v_a\\) on \\(X^*\\), and annihilates \\(dG\\). At \\(A\\), this follows from \\(G(0,u)=0\\).</p><p>Peano’s theorem gives local solutions of \\(\\dot z=W_a(z)\\), unique off \\(A\\). Any solution meeting \\(A\\) has \\(G=0\\) throughout by the chain rule. If it left \\(A\\), a connected interval off \\(A\\) would yield a \\(v_a\\)-trajectory with finite-time endpoint on \\(A\\), contrary to the hypothesis. Reversing time excludes arrival from outside \\(A\\). On \\(A\\), the equation is\n\\[\n\\dot x=0,\\qquad\\dot u=a,\n\\]\nso uniqueness holds there too.</p><p>For continuity at \\(A\\), bound the continuous field on a compact neighborhood. Initial points in a smaller neighborhood have solutions on a common short interval within that compact set. For convergent initial points, solutions are uniformly bounded and equicontinuous. Every uniformly convergent subsequence satisfies the limiting integral equation. Uniqueness identifies its limit, hence the whole sequence converges. The argument also applies to negative time. Reverse flow gives continuous local inverses.</p><p>Write \\(u=s+it\\). From \\((x,0)\\), follow \\(W_1\\) for time \\(s\\), then \\(W_i\\) for time \\(t\\). Uniform short-time existence works on common smaller neighborhoods. The endpoint defines \\(H_u(x)\\), preserving \\(G\\) and the section. Reverse the steps in opposite order for the joint inverse. In a neighborhood where these reverse operations are defined, the image of the open source is its inverse image under the reverse map, hence open. At \\(u=0\\), both times are zero.</p><p>Under the stronger trajectory hypothesis, integrate\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(u)W_1+\\operatorname{Im}(u)W_i\n\\]\nfor time one. The uniqueness argument applies to the combined field. Its speed on a compact neighborhood is bounded by a constant times \\(|u|\\), so solutions exist to time one after shrinking. Compactness for the integral equations, allowing \\(u\\) to vary, gives joint continuity; reverse transport gives the inverse.</p><p>The small-o assumption and trajectory control have different roles. The Lê–Saito–Teissier criterion supplies the small-o estimate for the \\(\\mu\\)-constant isolated families considered here [20; 28, Chapter II, Remark 3.10]. The full-gradient version is equivalent because\n\\[\n\\|dG\\|^2=\\|d_xG\\|^2+|G_u|^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Corollaries \\(B.3\\) and \\(B.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In either case, \\(X^*\\) admits fields satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition \\(11\\), and the trajectory condition holds for every constant real combination of the two fields.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G,A,X^*\\) satisfy the analytic and gradient hypotheses of Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. \\((X^*,A)\\) satisfies the Whitney conditions; or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. there is a proper holomorphic \\(b:M\\to\\{G=0\\}\\), isomorphic off \\(A\\), such that \\(q:M\\to D\\) is smooth and \\(E=b^{-1}(A)\\) is relative normal crossing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In either case, \\(X^*\\) admits fields satisfying the hypotheses of Proposition \\(11\\), and the trajectory condition holds for every constant real combination of the two fields. Consequently \\(G\\) has the jointly continuous ambient right-trivialization of that proposition.</p><p>In the Whitney case the fields can be chosen to preserve \\(\\|x\\|^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(12.1\\) supplies the fields and radial trajectory control under Whitney regularity. Lemma \\(12.2\\) supplies them from a proper smooth resolved zero family with relative normal crossings. Proposition \\(11\\) then gives the ambient maps.</p><p>For the families of Theorem \\(1\\), these are alternative constructions. In the double-point case, use the plane family after splitting off a square, extend by the identity, and conjugate back as in Proposition \\(10\\). In the log-canonical multiplicity-three case, use the Whitney construction directly. In the non-log-canonical case, use the resolved family of Proposition \\(8\\) after its finite disk cover.</p><p>The Lê–Saito–Teissier estimate applies to these \\(\\mu\\)-constant families. The closed-ball construction of Proposition \\(9\\) additionally supplies the exact common tube domains used to remove the finite cover.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Corollary \\(B.3\\), “An explicit Whitney lift.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On \\(X^*\\) approaching \\(A\\), \\( \\frac{\\|q(x,u)\\|}{\\|x\\|}\\longrightarrow1. \\) Using \\(k_a\\) from Proposition \\(11\\), the fields …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the analytic and gradient hypotheses of Proposition \\(12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((X^*,A)\\) is Whitney regular.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal K=\\ker d_xG\\), let the Hermitian inner product be linear in its first entry, and put\n  \\[\n  q(x,u)=\\pi_{\\mathcal K_{(x,u)}}x.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On \\(X^*\\) approaching \\(A\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|q(x,u)\\|}{\\|x\\|}\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]\nUsing \\(k_a\\) from Proposition \\(11\\), the fields\n\\[\nw_a\n=\nk_a-\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}\\langle k_a,x\\rangle}{\\|q\\|^2}q,\n\\qquad\nv_a=(w_a,a),\\qquad a=1,i,\n\\]\nare defined sufficiently near \\(A\\), satisfy the continuous-lift criterion, and preserve \\(\\|x\\|^2\\). Every constant real combination also preserves \\(\\|x\\|^2\\). Hence \\(G\\) has the trivialization of Proposition \\(11\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Along any sequence, pass to a subsequence with\n\\[\nx/\\|x\\|\\to v,\\qquad \\mathcal K_{(x,u)}\\to Q.\n\\]\nThe gradient estimate implies that tangent spaces of the total zero set tend to \\(Q\\times\\mathbb C\\). Whitney \\((b)\\), applied to secants from \\((0,u)\\), puts \\((v,0)\\) in that space. Hence \\(v\\in Q\\), proving\n\\[\n\\|q\\|/\\|x\\|\\to1.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(q\\ne0\\) near the section.</p><p>The correction in the displayed definition of \\(w_a\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal K\\), so\n\\[\ndG(v_a)=0.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\langle q,x\\rangle=\\|q\\|^2,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nd\\|x\\|^2(v_a)=0.\n\\]\nThe correction norm is bounded by\n\\[\n\\|k_a\\|\\frac{\\|x\\|}{\\|q\\|},\n\\]\nso \\(w_a\\to0\\) by the gradient estimate and the proved ratio.</p><p>Every constant real combination preserves a trajectory’s positive radius. No such trajectory reaches \\(A\\) in finite time. Apply Proposition \\(11\\), including its radial-transport assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Corollary \\(B.4\\), “Continuous lifts from a resolution.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The punctured zero family admits smooth fields \\(v_1,v_i\\) whose spatial parts tend to zero at \\(A\\), and no trajectory of any constant real combination has a finite-time endpoint in \\(A\\) inside the representative.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the analytic and gradient hypotheses of Proposition \\(12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume a proper holomorphic map\n  \\[\n  b:M\\to\\{G=0\\}\n  \\]\n  is an isomorphism off \\(A\\), \\(q:M\\to D\\) is smooth, and \\(E=b^{-1}(A)\\) is relative normal crossing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The punctured zero family admits smooth fields \\(v_1,v_i\\) whose spatial parts tend to zero at \\(A\\), and no trajectory of any constant real combination has a finite-time endpoint in \\(A\\) inside the representative. Hence \\(G\\) has the trivialization of Proposition \\(11\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Relative normal-crossing coordinates provide smooth lifts of the real parameter directions \\(1,i\\), tangent to each local branch of \\(E\\). Submersion coordinates provide lifts elsewhere. Patch by a partition of unity to obtain smooth fields \\(\\widetilde v_1,\\widetilde v_i\\) on \\(M\\) with the same projections and tangencies. Push them through the isomorphism off \\(E\\), obtaining \\(v_a=(w_a,a)\\) on \\(X^*\\).</p><p>Their spatial parts tend to zero at \\(A\\). Properness supplies convergent subsequences of unique preimages of any sequence in \\(X^*\\) tending to a point of \\(A\\). Every limit lies in \\(E\\). The spatial component of \\(b\\) vanishes on \\(E\\), so its differential on a field tangent to a branch of \\(E\\) is zero there. Continuity of \\(db(\\widetilde v_a)\\) gives \\(w_a\\to0\\).</p><p>Suppose an integral arc of a constant real combination of the \\(v_a\\) had a finite-time endpoint in \\(A\\). Its lift to \\(M\\setminus E\\) solves the corresponding smooth combined field. A terminal downstairs segment has compact closure; properness makes its inverse image compact. Smooth ODE continuation extends the lifted arc to a point of \\(E\\). Tangency preserves \\(E\\), and uniqueness in both directions prevents an arc outside \\(E\\) from reaching it. This contradiction proves trajectory control.</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(11\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [4, 3, "part"], [5, 3, "part"], [6, 3, "part"], [7, 3, "part"], [8, 3, "part"], [10, 9, "part"], [12, 11, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [20, 17, "part"], [21, 17, "part"], [23, 22, "part"], [26, 25, "part"], [27, 25, "part"], [28, 25, "part"], [29, 25, "part"], [33, 32, "part"], [34, 32, "part"]]});
